window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("backend-fundamentals", {"topics":[{"key":"backend-fundamentals/python","sources":["Official docs: Python (asyncio, concurrent.futures)","PEP 703 — Making the Global Interpreter Lock Optional in CPython","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"An AI service is glue: it takes a request, builds a prompt, calls a model (or several), validates the\nstructured answer, maybe hits a vector store and a tokenizer along the way, and streams a result back.\nEach of those pieces exists first, and usually only, as a Python library: PyTorch, Hugging Face,\nsentence-transformers, tiktoken, every provider's official SDK, the eval harnesses, the notebooks the\nresearch team used to find the prompt that works. Choosing another language for the backend means\nreimplementing those pieces, shelling out to Python anyway, or maintaining two stacks.\n\nThe obvious objection is \"Python is slow, serve it in Go or Java\". It fails on arithmetic. A model call\ntakes hundreds of milliseconds to tens of seconds; the request-handling overhead that a faster\nlanguage would shave is measured in microseconds. The hot loops that matter, matrix multiplies and\ntokenization, already run in C, C++, Rust or CUDA underneath the Python API. What Python must do well\nis wait on many model calls at once, and `asyncio` does exactly that.\n\nThe opposite objection, \"Python cannot do concurrency because of the GIL\", fails for the same reason:\nthe GIL is released while a thread is blocked on a socket, and an event loop does not need threads at\nall to hold thousands of in-flight requests. The GIL is a real limit only for CPU-bound pure-Python\nloops, and the answer there is a native library or a separate process, not a different language.","how":"### Execution model and the GIL\nCPython compiles source to bytecode and interprets it. The Global Interpreter Lock lets only one\nthread execute Python bytecode at a time *because* the reference-counting memory model is not\nthread-safe without it. Threads still help for I/O: the interpreter drops the GIL during blocking\nsystem calls, and well-behaved C extensions (NumPy, PyTorch, hashing, compression) release it during\nlong native work. For CPU-bound pure-Python code, use `multiprocessing` or `ProcessPoolExecutor`, which\nsidestep the GIL by running separate interpreters. Python 3.13 added an experimental free-threaded\nbuild (PEP 703) that removes the GIL, but it is opt-in and the ecosystem is still catching up, so plan\nas if the GIL is there.\n\n### asyncio: waiting without threads\nAn `async def` function is a coroutine; `await` hands control back to the event loop until the awaited\nI/O completes. One thread can therefore hold hundreds of pending model calls, each costing a small\nobject rather than a thread stack. This is the shape of an LLM backend: fan out calls, cap concurrency\nwith a `Semaphore` to respect provider rate limits, gather results. The one rule that matters is that\na blocking call inside a coroutine (a sync HTTP client, `time.sleep`, a heavy loop) freezes the whole\nloop, because nothing else can run until it returns.\n\n### Types and Pydantic\nType hints are annotations; the interpreter does not enforce them. That is fine inside your code with\n`mypy` checking it, but data crossing a boundary (an HTTP body, a model's JSON output, a config file)\nneeds runtime validation. Pydantic models validate and coerce at runtime and emit JSON Schema, so one\nclass can be the API contract, the structured-output schema you hand to an LLM, and the parsed result.\nThis single-source-of-truth role is why Pydantic became the lingua franca of AI services.\n\n### Packaging and environments\nEach project gets a virtual environment and a lockfile; `uv`, Poetry or plain `pip` with a\nrequirements lock all work. The characteristic pain is heavy native dependencies (PyTorch with a\nspecific CUDA build), which is why AI services pin exact versions and ship in containers.\n\n### Where it sits in the stack\nTypically: a FastAPI service for the API, worker processes for long jobs, scripts and notebooks for\nevals and data prep. The product UI and sometimes a thin gateway live in TypeScript. The boundary\nbetween them is an HTTP contract, usually generated from the Python service's OpenAPI schema.","example":"Fan out a batch of classification calls concurrently with a concurrency cap, validate each model\nanswer at runtime, and push a CPU-bound step out of the GIL-bound main process. The `Semaphore` is the\nrate-limit guard; the `ProcessPoolExecutor` is the GIL escape hatch.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\nimport httpx\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError\n\nclass Verdict(BaseModel):\n    label: str\n    confidence: float\n\nSEM = asyncio.Semaphore(8)                      # at most 8 model calls in flight\n\nasync def classify(client: httpx.AsyncClient, text: str) -> Verdict | None:\n    async with SEM:                             # waits without holding a thread\n        r = await client.post(\"http://localhost:8000/v1/classify\", json={\"text\": text}, timeout=30)\n    try:\n        return Verdict.model_validate(r.json())  # runtime check of the model's structured output\n    except ValidationError:\n        return None\n\ndef count_words(text: str) -> int:              # stand-in for CPU-heavy pure-Python work\n    return sum(1 for _ in text.split())\n\nasync def main(texts: list[str]):\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient() as client:   # one connection pool for the whole batch\n        verdicts = await asyncio.gather(*(classify(client, t) for t in texts))\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor() as pool:         # CPU-bound work runs in separate interpreters\n        counts = await asyncio.gather(*(loop.run_in_executor(pool, count_words, t) for t in texts))\n    return list(zip(verdicts, counts))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":                      # required for process pools on macOS/Windows\n    print(asyncio.run(main([\"refund my order\", \"great product\"] * 50)))\n```","tradeoffs":["You get the entire ML ecosystem and the fastest path from notebook to service, but you pay with a runtime that is slow for CPU-bound pure-Python code; this flips only when your service does heavy per-request computation in Python itself rather than in native libraries or a model.","`asyncio` gives cheap concurrency for I/O-bound work, but every library on the request path must be async-aware; one blocking call undoes it, and mixing sync and async code is a constant tax.","Dynamic typing speeds experimentation, but production code needs `mypy` plus runtime validation at boundaries; skipping either shows up as type errors discovered by users instead of CI.","Rich native dependencies (PyTorch, CUDA) are the ecosystem's strength and its deployment weakness: images are large and version pinning is non-negotiable."],"pitfalls":["Calling a synchronous client (`requests`, a sync DB driver) inside an `async def` handler; the symptom is throughput collapsing to one request at a time while CPU sits idle.","Treating the GIL as the reason a service is slow when it is I/O-bound; profiling shows threads blocked on sockets, and the fix is concurrency, not multiprocessing.","Trusting type hints as validation; a model returns `\"confidence\": \"high\"` and the code crashes three functions later instead of at the boundary.","Unpinned dependencies in an AI service; a transitive upgrade of a tokenizer or SDK changes outputs and the symptom is evals drifting with no code change."],"when":"Default to Python for anything that touches models, tokenizers, embeddings, evals or data preparation,\nand for the service layer that orchestrates them: the library support decides it. Keep CPU-bound hot\npaths in native code or separate processes, and measure before assuming Python is the bottleneck;\nin most LLM services the model call dominates by two or three orders of magnitude. Consider another\nlanguage for the product edge (TypeScript next to the UI) or for a tiny, extremely high-throughput\nproxy where microseconds matter. In interviews, the expected depth is explaining the GIL precisely\n(what it blocks, when it is released), why `asyncio` fits LLM workloads, and why Pydantic sits at\nevery boundary. The `python-for-ai` module covers each of these in detail.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes async service code, keeps blocking work off the event loop, validates every boundary with Pydantic, and pins the environment so evals stay reproducible.","architect":"Decides the language split: Python for the model core and data work, TypeScript or another language where the product or throughput argument wins, joined by a typed contract.","fde":"Lands inside a customer's existing Python (or non-Python) stack and must explain why the AI core stays Python and how it integrates with their Java or .NET services over HTTP."},"check":{"q":"A colleague argues the new LLM gateway should be written in Go because \"Python's GIL cannot handle\n2,000 concurrent requests\". How do you respond, and what would change your answer?","a":"The GIL serialises Python bytecode execution, not waiting. A gateway's work per request is\nbuilding a payload and awaiting a model call that takes seconds; with `asyncio`, one process holds\nthousands of pending awaits on a single thread, and the GIL is irrelevant while they wait on sockets.\nMultiple uvicorn workers add CPU headroom for serialisation. I would ask what CPU-bound work the\ngateway does per request; if it must tokenize or transform megabytes in pure Python per call, that is\nthe argument for native code or another language. I would also weigh the cost of a second language\nwhen the model, eval and SDK ecosystem is Python-first."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the GIL rarely limit an LLM-serving backend?","options":["Modern Python releases the GIL for all threads automatically","The service is I/O-bound, and the GIL is released while waiting on sockets, so asyncio or threads overlap the waits","Uvicorn runs each request in its own interpreter","LLM responses are small, so bytecode execution is negligible regardless of concurrency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The GIL blocks concurrent execution of Python bytecode, not concurrent waiting. An LLM backend mostly awaits network I/O, during which the GIL is not held, so one process serves many requests."},{"q":"Which piece of work should leave the main Python process rather than run inside a coroutine?","options":["Awaiting 200 concurrent HTTP calls to a model provider","A NumPy matrix multiply over a batch of embeddings","A pure-Python loop that parses a 500 MB log file line by line","Reading configuration from environment variables at startup"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pure-Python CPU work holds the GIL and blocks the event loop; a process pool runs it in separate interpreters. NumPy releases the GIL in native code, and awaited HTTP calls are I/O-bound."},{"q":"What does a Pydantic model add that a type hint alone does not?","options":["It makes the interpreter reject wrongly typed arguments at call time","Runtime validation and coercion of external data plus JSON Schema generation","Faster attribute access through compiled slots","Automatic retries when validation fails"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Type hints are inert annotations checked only by tools like mypy. Pydantic validates data at runtime, which is what an HTTP body or a model's JSON output needs, and emits the schema you give the LLM."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/fastapi","sources":["Official docs: FastAPI","Official docs: Starlette","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"An AI endpoint is unusual HTTP. A request may hold its connection open for ten seconds, stream\nhundreds of chunks, carry a large structured body in and a large structured body out, and share\nexpensive clients (a model-provider connection pool, a vector store) across every request. The\nclassic Python web stack was built for the opposite: WSGI frameworks like Flask and traditional\nDjango dedicate one thread or process to a request from start to finish. With 200 in-flight model\ncalls of five seconds each, a WSGI server needs 200 workers to avoid queueing, and those workers sit\nidle on sockets. An ASGI server runs one event loop per process that holds all 200 pending awaits at\nnegligible cost, so concurrency is bounded by memory and the upstream, not by worker count.\n\nThe second forcing problem is contract drift. Hand-written validation and hand-written API docs\ndiverge the week after launch; a client sends `temperature: \"0.7\"` and the handler crashes deep in a\nprovider call. FastAPI reads the Pydantic types on the handler signature and derives validation, a\nprecise 422 error, response filtering and the OpenAPI schema from one declaration, so the docs cannot\nlie about the code. In AI services that same model class is also the JSON Schema you hand a model for\nstructured output, which closes the loop between API contract and prompt contract.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"use raw Starlette or aiohttp and validate by hand\", works but reinvents those\nlayers, and Flask-with-async-bolted-on keeps the WSGI mental model where it hurts most.","how":"### ASGI and the request path\nUvicorn accepts the connection and hands an ASGI `scope` to the app. The request passes through the\nmiddleware stack (CORS, auth, tracing), the router matches the path and method, FastAPI resolves the\nhandler's dependencies, validates path, query and body parameters against the declared types, calls\nthe handler, then serialises the return value through `response_model`. Validation failures stop at\nthe boundary with a 422 and a field-level error list, before any of your code runs.\n\n### `async def` versus `def` handlers\nAn `async def` handler runs directly on the event loop, so every `await` yields to other requests. A\nplain `def` handler is run in a bounded worker threadpool so that it cannot block the loop. The choice\nis deliberate: use `async def` when everything you call is async (httpx, async DB drivers, provider\nSDKs' async clients); use `def` when you must call a blocking library. The failure mode is the\nmix: a sync SDK call inside `async def` blocks the loop and every other request on that worker waits.\n\n### Pydantic as the contract\nDeclaring `body: ChatRequest` validates and parses the JSON body; `response_model=Answer` filters the\noutput to the declared fields (so a leaked internal attribute never reaches a client) and documents\nit. `Field` constraints (`max_length`, `ge`, `le`) become both runtime checks and schema metadata.\n\n### Dependency injection and lifespan\n`Depends` lets a handler ask for a current user, a DB session or a shared HTTP client without\nconstructing it; dependencies compose and can be overridden in tests. `lifespan` runs startup and\nshutdown code, which is where you build application-scoped resources (one `httpx.AsyncClient`, one\nmodel client) instead of recreating them per request.\n\n### Streaming\n`StreamingResponse` takes an async generator and writes each yielded chunk as it arrives. With\n`text/event-stream` framing you get server-sent events, the standard way to stream tokens to browsers\nand SDKs. Because each `yield` happens between awaits, a slow stream does not block other requests.\n\n### Compared with the alternatives\nFlask is simpler and sync-first, fine for small internal tools but a poor fit for long-lived streaming\ncalls. Django brings an ORM, admin and auth; choose it when those matter more than async purity, and\nnote its async support is partial. Starlette is FastAPI without validation, DI and docs. Litestar is\na comparable ASGI framework with a different DI style; the reasoning in this lesson applies to it too.","example":"A streaming chat endpoint: one shared upstream client created in `lifespan`, a validated request\nbody, and an SSE stream produced by an async generator. Every `await` inside `events()` releases the\nloop to other requests, which is the whole point of the async design.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nimport httpx\nfrom fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Request\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app: FastAPI):\n    app.state.http = httpx.AsyncClient(base_url=\"http://model-server:8000\", timeout=60)\n    yield                                          # the app serves requests here\n    await app.state.http.aclose()                  # shutdown: close the pool cleanly\n\napp = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)\n\nclass ChatRequest(BaseModel):\n    prompt: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=8000)\n    temperature: float = Field(0.2, ge=0, le=2)   # validated and documented from one declaration\n\ndef http(request: Request) -> httpx.AsyncClient:   # dependency: one shared pool, not one per request\n    return request.app.state.http\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\")\nasync def chat(body: ChatRequest, client: httpx.AsyncClient = Depends(http)):\n    async def events():\n        async with client.stream(\"POST\", \"/generate\", json=body.model_dump()) as r:\n            async for line in r.aiter_lines():     # each await yields the loop to other requests\n                if line:\n                    yield f\"data: {json.dumps({'token': line})}\\n\\n\"\n        yield \"data: [DONE]\\n\\n\"\n    return StreamingResponse(events(), media_type=\"text/event-stream\")\n```","tradeoffs":["You gain high concurrency for I/O-bound handlers, but every library on the hot path must be async or explicitly offloaded; one blocking call turns the advantage into a liability.","Pydantic-driven validation removes boilerplate and keeps docs honest, but it adds per-request parsing cost; for very large bodies or extreme throughput that cost is measurable and worth profiling.","FastAPI is deliberately thin (no ORM, no admin, no auth framework), which keeps it flexible but means you assemble those pieces yourself; Django wins when a full-stack CRUD app is the actual job.","Single-process asyncio scales I/O, not CPU; you still run several uvicorn workers per machine and design them to be stateless."],"pitfalls":["Using a synchronous provider SDK or `requests` inside `async def`; the symptom is p99 latency that equals the slowest upstream call for every request on that worker.","Creating an `httpx.AsyncClient` per request; the symptom is a new TLS handshake per call and socket exhaustion under load, fixed by one client in `lifespan`.","Returning ORM objects or internal dicts without `response_model`; the symptom is an internal field (a cost figure, a secret flag) appearing in client responses.","Doing CPU-heavy work (tokenizing megabytes, re-ranking in pure Python) inside the handler; the symptom is event-loop stalls visible as every endpoint slowing together."],"when":"Reach for FastAPI when building the HTTP layer of a Python AI service: model gateways, RAG endpoints,\nagent runners, anything that awaits a model and benefits from streaming and typed contracts. Prefer\nDjango when the application is mostly conventional CRUD with an ORM and admin, and Flask only for\ntiny sync tools. Measure event-loop blocking (a handler that should take 50 ms taking seconds under\nload) and connection reuse. In day-to-day work the skill is keeping the request path fully async,\ndesigning Pydantic models that double as structured-output schemas, and wiring shared clients\nthrough dependencies; interviews probe whether you know why `def` and `async def` handlers behave\ndifferently. The `fastapi-for-ai` module covers each mechanism in depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds endpoints with Pydantic contracts, shared async clients via `Depends`, and SSE streaming, and keeps anything blocking out of the event loop.","architect":"Decides that the AI service layer is ASGI and stateless, sets worker and concurrency limits per process, and treats the OpenAPI schema as the published contract for other teams.","fde":"Stands up a FastAPI service inside a customer's network, exposes `/docs` so their integrators can self-serve, and explains why their sync middleware must not wrap async handlers."},"check":{"q":"A FastAPI service has one `async def` endpoint that calls a provider through a synchronous SDK.\nUnder light load it is fine; under load every endpoint, including the health check, becomes slow.\nExplain the mechanism and two fixes with their trade-offs.","a":"The sync SDK call blocks the event loop thread for the full duration of the model request, so\nno other coroutine on that worker, including the health check, can run until it returns; under load\nrequests queue behind each other. Fix one: switch to the SDK's async client (or httpx) so the call\nyields to the loop; this is the right long-term fix but requires the whole path to be async. Fix two:\nchange the handler to plain `def` so FastAPI runs it in the threadpool; this unblocks the loop\nimmediately but caps concurrency at the threadpool size and adds thread overhead. Either way, add a\nmetric for event-loop lag so a regression is visible."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does FastAPI run a plain `def` handler in a threadpool but an `async def` handler on the loop?","options":["Threadpools are faster for JSON serialisation","A sync function would block the single event loop, so it is isolated in a thread while coroutines yield at each await","Pydantic validation only works in threads","The threadpool enables HTTP/2 multiplexing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The event loop is one thread; a blocking function on it stops every other request. Running sync handlers in threads protects the loop, while async handlers cooperate by yielding at awaits."},{"q":"What do you get from declaring the request body as a Pydantic model?","options":["Automatic caching of identical request bodies","Guaranteed backward compatibility when fields are renamed","Runtime validation with a 422 error, parsed typed access, and the matching OpenAPI schema from one declaration","Automatic retries of the upstream model call on validation failure"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The handler's type annotations drive validation, parsing and documentation together, so the docs and the behaviour cannot diverge. Caching, compatibility and retries are separate concerns."},{"q":"When is Django a better choice than FastAPI for a service that also calls an LLM?","options":["When the service must stream tokens to a browser","When the application is mostly CRUD with an ORM, admin and auth, and the LLM call is a small part of it","When the service needs OpenAPI documentation","When the provider SDK is async-only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FastAPI's strengths are async I/O and typed contracts; Django's are batteries for conventional web apps. If the bulk of the work is CRUD and admin, Django's ecosystem outweighs async purity."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/node-js-typescript","sources":["Official docs: Node.js (event loop, streams, worker_threads)","Official docs: TypeScript handbook","Official docs: Zod"],"why":"AI products are mostly chat and streaming interfaces, and the web is built in TypeScript: React,\nNext.js and the server-side rendering frameworks that render them, the serverless and edge platforms\nthat host them, and the Vercel AI SDK that streams model output into components. A team shipping a\nproduct cannot avoid TypeScript, and putting the request path that talks to the model next to the UI\nin the same language removes a translation layer, a deployment and a second on-call rota.\n\nThe naive \"everything in Python\" plan fails at the edge: the frontend team does not write Python,\nSSR frameworks are TypeScript, and edge runtimes do not run CPython. The naive \"everything in\nTypeScript\" plan fails at the core: training, fine-tuning, evaluation harnesses, tokenizers, vector\nand data tooling are Python-first, and rewriting them is not a project any product team wants. The\nhonest outcome is a two-language system, and the real engineering question becomes where the boundary\nsits and how its contract is kept honest.\n\nNode fits the edge role for a mechanical reason. Its single-threaded event loop with non-blocking I/O\nhandles thousands of concurrent streaming connections cheaply, which is what a chat gateway does all\nday: open an upstream stream, relay chunks, apply backpressure, close. What it does badly is the same\nthing Python does badly, CPU-bound work on the main thread, and it has no GIL-free native ML stack to\nhand that work to.","how":"### The event loop\nJavaScript runs on one thread; libuv provides a thread pool for file, DNS and crypto work and a loop\nthat dispatches completed I/O callbacks. A request handler that awaits a network call costs a promise,\nnot a thread, so concurrency is cheap. The corollary is that any synchronous CPU work on the main\nthread (parsing a 50 MB JSON body, tokenizing a book in pure JavaScript) stops every other request.\nThe escape hatches are `worker_threads` for CPU-bound tasks and keeping heavy compute in the Python\ncore, *because* nothing on the main thread can preempt a running function.\n\n### Streams and backpressure\nNode 18 and later ship `fetch` and the Web Streams API globally, so a handler can read an upstream\nmodel stream chunk by chunk and write it to the client as it arrives. Writes return `false` when the\nclient's buffer is full; honouring that signal (wait for `drain`) is what keeps memory flat when a\nslow browser consumes a fast model, and ignoring it is the classic way a streaming proxy runs out of\nmemory.\n\n### TypeScript: static types, no runtime\nTypeScript's types are erased at compile time. Inside your code they catch mistakes; at the boundary\nthey guarantee nothing, *because* the data arrives at runtime from a model, a webhook or an API that\nnever saw your types. That is why TypeScript AI code pairs a runtime schema library (Zod and similar)\nwith inferred static types: one schema validates the LLM's JSON, derives the TypeScript type, and can\nbe converted to JSON Schema for the model's tool definition. `strict` mode and exhaustive switches on\ndiscriminated unions are the other half of the discipline.\n\n### Where it fits in an AI system\nTypical Node/TS responsibilities: the chat UI and its streaming route handlers, a backend-for-frontend\nthat adds auth and rate limits in front of the Python service, serverless functions and edge\nmiddleware, MCP servers exposing product data as tools, and agent orchestration through the OpenAI,\nAnthropic and Vercel SDKs. Typical Python responsibilities: model serving, RAG pipelines, evals,\nfine-tuning, batch data jobs. The boundary is an HTTP (often SSE) contract; generating the TypeScript\nclient from the Python service's OpenAPI schema is how the two sides stay in sync.","example":"A minimal Node gateway that relays a streaming response from the Python core while honouring\nbackpressure, plus a Zod schema that validates a model-produced tool call at runtime and provides the\nstatic type for free.\n\n```ts\nimport http from \"node:http\";\nimport { Readable } from \"node:stream\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst ToolCall = z.object({                       // runtime schema: TS types vanish at runtime\n  name: z.literal(\"lookup_order\"),\n  arguments: z.object({ orderId: z.string().regex(/^ord_[a-z0-9]+$/) }),\n});\ntype ToolCall = z.infer<typeof ToolCall>;        // static type derived from the runtime schema\n\nasync function* relay(upstream: Response): AsyncGenerator<string> {\n  const reader = upstream.body!.getReader();\n  const dec = new TextDecoder();\n  for (;;) {\n    const { value, done } = await reader.read();  // yields the event loop between chunks\n    if (done) return;\n    yield dec.decode(value, { stream: true });\n  }\n}\n\nhttp.createServer(async (req, res) => {\n  const upstream = await fetch(\"http://python-core:8000/v1/chat\", {\n    method: \"POST\",\n    headers: { \"content-type\": \"application/json\" },\n    body: Readable.toWeb(req) as ReadableStream,  // forward the client body without buffering it\n    duplex: \"half\",\n  } as RequestInit);\n  res.writeHead(200, { \"content-type\": \"text/event-stream\" });\n  for await (const chunk of relay(upstream)) {\n    if (!res.write(chunk)) await new Promise((r) => res.once(\"drain\", r)); // honour backpressure\n  }\n  res.end();\n}).listen(3000);\n\nconst parsed = ToolCall.safeParse(JSON.parse('{\"name\":\"lookup_order\",\"arguments\":{\"orderId\":\"ord_42x\"}}'));\nif (!parsed.success) console.error(parsed.error.issues);   // never trust model output structurally\n```","tradeoffs":["One language from browser to gateway cuts translation and team overhead, but the model, eval and data ecosystem stays in Python, so you pay for a cross-language contract instead.","The event loop makes streaming proxies cheap, but any CPU-bound step on the main thread blocks everyone; the balance flips when per-request compute grows and you need worker threads or Python.","Static types give refactoring safety, but they vanish at runtime, so every external boundary needs a schema library and the duplication that comes with it.","Serverless and edge deployment is frictionless for Node, but long-running agent loops and large streaming responses hit platform timeouts and body-size limits that a long-lived server never sees."],"pitfalls":["Parsing and validating model output with a TypeScript cast (`as ToolCall`); the symptom is a runtime `undefined is not a function` far from the boundary that produced the bad data.","Buffering a whole model stream before writing it to the client; the symptom is time-to-first-token equal to total generation time and memory spikes under concurrent chats.","Running tokenization or large JSON transforms synchronously in a route handler; the symptom is every concurrent request's latency rising together, visible as event-loop lag.","Letting the Python service's API and the hand-written TypeScript client drift; the symptom is a field rename on one side surfacing as silent `undefined` on the other."],"when":"Use Node/TypeScript for the layer that faces the product: UI streaming routes, a backend-for-frontend,\nserverless glue, MCP servers for product data, and lightweight agent orchestration where the SDKs are\nexcellent. Keep inference, evaluation, fine-tuning and data pipelines in Python. If a team is entirely\nTypeScript and the AI work is API calls to a provider, a TypeScript-only stack is reasonable until\nevals or custom models appear. Measure event-loop lag and streaming memory; generate the client from\nOpenAPI rather than typing it by hand. In interviews, expect to explain why types are erased, how\nbackpressure works in a streaming proxy, and where the Python boundary belongs. The `node-ts-for-ai`\nmodule goes deep on each.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes streaming route handlers, validates model output with Zod, keeps CPU work off the main thread, and generates the client types from the Python service's OpenAPI schema.","architect":"Draws the TypeScript/Python boundary, chooses between a BFF and direct calls, and owns the contract-generation pipeline that keeps the two sides from drifting.","fde":"Meets customers whose product teams are TypeScript-only and must show how the AI core integrates through SSE and typed clients without forcing a Python rewrite on them."},"check":{"q":"A customer's TypeScript team wants to build the entire AI feature, including evals and a\nretrieval pipeline, in Node to avoid \"a second language\". What do you tell them, and where would you\ndraw the line?","a":"I would separate the edge from the core. The chat route, streaming, auth and the MCP tools can\nlive in TypeScript today with first-class SDKs, and that is where their team is strongest. Evals,\nembedding pipelines, reranking and any fine-tuning depend on Python-first libraries and the research\nworkflow; rewriting them in Node means maintaining ports of tools that move monthly. The line is\n\"anything that touches model weights, tokenizers or evaluation datasets is Python; anything that\ntouches the user is TypeScript\", with an OpenAPI-generated client as the contract so the second\nlanguage costs a build step rather than a translation team."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must a TypeScript service validate an LLM's JSON output with a runtime schema even though the response type is declared?","options":["Because JSON.parse returns `any` only in strict mode","TypeScript types are erased at compile time, so declared types guarantee nothing about data that arrives at runtime","Because LLM output is always a string and must be converted","Because Node's fetch strips type information from responses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Static types describe what the compiler assumes, not what the network delivers. A Zod-style schema checks the bytes that actually arrived and derives the static type from the same definition."},{"q":"Which operation blocks every other request in a Node gateway?","options":["Awaiting a 5-second streaming response from the model provider","Writing chunks to 500 concurrent SSE clients","Synchronously parsing a 50 MB JSON document in the route handler","Reading a secret from an environment variable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Node runs JavaScript on one thread; synchronous CPU work cannot be interrupted, so all pending callbacks wait. Awaited I/O and buffered writes yield to the loop between operations."},{"q":"In a mixed TypeScript/Python AI stack, which responsibility belongs in the Python core?","options":["The streaming chat route handler next to the React UI","Edge middleware that adds auth headers","The evaluation harness and embedding pipeline","An MCP server exposing the product's CRM records as tools"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Evals, embeddings and anything near model weights depend on Python-first tooling. Routes, middleware and MCP servers for product data are edge concerns where TypeScript's SDKs and team fit win."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/rest-graphql-api-design","sources":["Fielding, 2000 — Architectural Styles and the Design of Network-based Software Architectures","IETF RFC 9110 — HTTP Semantics","IETF RFC 9457 — Problem Details for HTTP APIs","Official docs: GraphQL specification"],"why":"The moment a client ships against your interface, that interface is frozen. Design decisions made\ncasually on day one, an action-style endpoint named `/getUserOrders2`, an error returned as 200 with\n`{\"ok\": false}`, a delete that is not safe to retry, cost forever. API styles exist to make those\ndecisions by convention instead of by accident.\n\nREST's insight is to lean on HTTP rather than tunnel through it. If `GET` is safe and idempotent,\ncaches can store it and clients can retry it blind. If `PUT` and `DELETE` are idempotent, a timeout\ncan be retried without creating duplicates. If status codes carry meaning, load balancers can count\n5xx responses and retry policies can act on 429 and 503 without reading bodies. The naive alternative,\none `POST /api` endpoint with an `action` field in the body, throws all of that away: nothing can be\ncached, nothing is retry-safe by inspection, and every request looks identical to observability.\n\nGraphQL arose at Facebook (open-sourced in 2015) from a different pain: mobile clients needed many\ndifferent shapes of the same data, and REST either over-fetched (one fat resource) or under-fetched\n(three round trips to assemble a screen), with endpoints multiplying per screen. A typed schema plus\nclient-authored queries fixes that by moving shape selection to the client. The cost is giving up\nHTTP's per-resource semantics, which is why neither style dominates.\n\nFor AI services the question is sharper: model calls are big `POST` bodies with streaming responses,\nand tools exposed to an agent are easiest to describe as REST operations with an OpenAPI schema.","how":"### REST: resources, methods, status codes\nModel nouns, not verbs: `/conversations/{id}/messages`, not `/sendMessage`. Collections and items\nnest at most a level or two. Methods carry semantics defined by HTTP: `GET` is safe and idempotent and\nmay be cached; `PUT` replaces a resource and is idempotent; `DELETE` is idempotent; `PATCH` applies a\npartial change; `POST` creates or triggers and is *not* idempotent, which is why non-trivial APIs\naccept an `Idempotency-Key` header on it. Status codes are the first thing a client branches on:\n`200` and `201 Created`, `202 Accepted` for work that continues after the response, `204` for empty\nsuccess, `400`/`422` for invalid input, `401`/`403` for identity and permission, `404`, `409` for state\nconflicts, `429` for rate limits, `5xx` for your faults. Use one error envelope everywhere; Problem\nDetails (RFC 9457) gives a standard JSON shape with `type`, `title`, `status` and `detail`.\n\nLong-running AI work maps cleanly: `POST /summaries` returns `202` with a `Location` to a job resource\nthat clients `GET` until it is `succeeded`, or the response itself streams as `text/event-stream`.\nBoth keep proxies and timeouts happy *because* no single request has to live as long as the job.\n\n### GraphQL: schema, resolvers, one endpoint\nA schema in SDL declares types and fields and three roots: `Query`, `Mutation`, `Subscription`. A\nclient sends a query naming exactly the fields it wants; the server executes it by calling a resolver\nper field, walking the tree. Because resolvers run per field and per parent object, a list of 100\nconversations each resolving `lastMessage` naively issues 101 database queries; the standard fix is a\nper-request DataLoader that batches and caches lookups. Everything travels as `POST` to one endpoint,\nso HTTP caching does not apply unless you adopt persisted queries over `GET`. Errors are returned\nalongside partial data in an `errors` array with a 200 status, which clients must inspect. Because\nclients author queries, the server must enforce depth and cost limits, *because* an unbounded nested\nquery is a denial-of-service vector. Subscriptions stream over WebSockets.\n\n### The decision\nAsk who the clients are and how many shapes they need. One product UI with screens assembled from a\nrich domain, owned by a team that iterates weekly: GraphQL pays off. Service-to-service calls, public\nAPIs for integrators, anything cached by CDNs, anything exposed to an LLM as a tool: REST with an\nOpenAPI schema, because the schema generates clients and tool definitions directly. Many teams run\nboth: GraphQL in the product's BFF, REST underneath and outward.","example":"A REST design for a long-running summarisation job in FastAPI: `POST` returns `202` with a `Location`,\n`GET` is safe to poll, `DELETE` is idempotent. The GraphQL SDL after it expresses the same domain for a\nclient that wants to pick fields and subscribe to updates.\n\n```python\nfrom uuid import uuid4\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Response, status\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\napp = FastAPI()\nJOBS: dict[str, dict] = {}                          # stand-in for a database\n\nclass SummarizeRequest(BaseModel):\n    document_id: str\n    max_words: int = 200\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/summaries\", status_code=status.HTTP_202_ACCEPTED)\nasync def create_summary(body: SummarizeRequest, response: Response):\n    job_id = f\"sum_{uuid4().hex[:12]}\"\n    JOBS[job_id] = {\"id\": job_id, \"status\": \"queued\", \"input\": body.model_dump(), \"result\": None}\n    # enqueue to a worker here; the HTTP request returns immediately\n    response.headers[\"Location\"] = f\"/v1/summaries/{job_id}\"   # where to poll\n    return JOBS[job_id]\n\n@app.get(\"/v1/summaries/{job_id}\")                  # GET is safe and idempotent: poll freely\nasync def get_summary(job_id: str):\n    job = JOBS.get(job_id)\n    if job is None:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail={\"type\": \"not_found\", \"job_id\": job_id})\n    return job                                       # 200 whether queued or done; status is in the body\n\n@app.delete(\"/v1/summaries/{job_id}\", status_code=204)   # idempotent: a second DELETE is also 204\nasync def cancel_summary(job_id: str):\n    JOBS.pop(job_id, None)\n    return Response(status_code=204)\n```\n\n```graphql\nenum JobStatus { QUEUED RUNNING SUCCEEDED FAILED }\ntype Summary { id: ID!  status: JobStatus!  result: String }\ntype Query { summary(id: ID!): Summary }\ntype Mutation { createSummary(documentId: ID!, maxWords: Int = 200): Summary! }\ntype Subscription { summaryUpdated(id: ID!): Summary! }\n```","tradeoffs":["REST gets HTTP caching, retry-safety by method and tooling for free, but a screen needing five resources costs five round trips; this flips to GraphQL when client-side shape variety dominates.","GraphQL gives clients exact shapes and one evolving schema, but you give up HTTP caching, must defend against expensive queries and must batch resolvers; it flips back to REST for public APIs.","Returning `202` and a job resource keeps requests short and proxy-friendly, but clients must poll or subscribe, which costs more code than one long request when jobs are reliably under a few seconds.","A single error envelope and strict status codes make clients simple, but they force you to decide the code for every failure up front rather than leaking exceptions as 500s."],"pitfalls":["Verbs in URLs and everything as `POST`; the symptom is that nothing can be cached or safely retried and observability shows one undifferentiated endpoint.","Returning errors with status 200 and a flag in the body; the symptom is retry and alerting policies that never fire and SDKs that treat failures as success.","GraphQL resolvers without DataLoader; the symptom is query count scaling with list length in the database logs (the N+1 pattern).","Holding a request open for a multi-minute AI job; the symptom is intermittent 504s from a proxy whose idle timeout you never configured, and clients retrying work that already ran.","Leaving GraphQL query depth and cost unlimited; the symptom is one client's nested query pinning the database while everything else times out."],"when":"Default to REST with an OpenAPI schema for AI service endpoints, internal service-to-service calls\nand anything an agent will call as a tool; the schema becomes the client, the docs and the tool\ndefinition. Reach for GraphQL when a product team owns a UI that assembles many shapes from a shared\ndomain and is paying for it in round trips and endpoint sprawl. Measure round trips per screen,\ncache hit rate and p99 latency before switching either way. Day to day, the work is naming resources,\nchoosing status codes deliberately and designing the long-running-job pattern. In interviews, expect\n\"design the API for X\" questions that test whether you reach for idempotency, `202` plus polling or\nstreaming, and a consistent error shape without being prompted.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Designs resource paths and status codes, implements `202` plus polling or SSE for model calls, and keeps the OpenAPI schema accurate enough to generate clients and tool definitions.","architect":"Decides which style each boundary uses (GraphQL in the BFF, REST between services and outward), sets the error envelope and idempotency conventions, and reviews breaking changes.","fde":"Maps a customer's existing REST or GraphQL conventions onto the AI service's contract and explains why model endpoints need async job patterns their CRUD APIs never required."},"check":{"q":"You are designing the API for a document-analysis service whose jobs take 30 seconds to 5\nminutes and whose results are read by a web UI, a partner integration and an internal agent. Which\nstyle and request pattern do you choose, and why?","a":"REST with an OpenAPI schema, because three different clients including an agent need a\ncontract that generates clients and tool definitions, and partners expect it. `POST /analyses` returns\n`202 Accepted` with a `Location` and a job resource; clients `GET` it until `status` is terminal, or\nsubscribe to an SSE stream of progress events for the UI. The `POST` accepts an `Idempotency-Key` so a\npartner's retry after a timeout does not run the job twice. Results live at `/analyses/{id}/result`\nand are cacheable once terminal. I would not pick GraphQL here: the shapes are few, the clients are\nmostly machines, and long-running work plus caching favour HTTP semantics."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why return `202 Accepted` with a `Location` header for a five-minute AI job instead of holding the request open until it finishes?","options":["Because `200` cannot carry a JSON body larger than the proxy buffer","Proxies and clients have idle timeouts, and a short request plus a pollable job resource survives them and is retry-safe","Because `202` enables HTTP caching of the eventual result","Because FastAPI cannot await for more than 60 seconds"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Long-held connections hit proxy and client timeouts and make retries ambiguous. A job resource turns the work into state that can be polled, cached when terminal and cancelled."},{"q":"Why can an ordinary HTTP cache not store GraphQL responses by default?","options":["GraphQL responses are always compressed","The GraphQL spec forbids caching","Queries go as `POST` to one endpoint, so the URL does not identify the resource being read","GraphQL responses never include a `Content-Type` header"],"answer":2,"explanation":"HTTP caches key on method and URL. With every query a `POST` to `/graphql`, there is nothing to key on; persisted queries over `GET` are the workaround."},{"q":"A GraphQL list of 100 conversations resolving `lastMessage` issues 101 database queries. What is the standard fix?","options":["Switch the field to a REST endpoint","A per-request DataLoader that batches and caches the lookups","Increase the database connection pool to 101","Mark the field as `@deprecated`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Resolvers run per parent object, so without batching each one queries alone. DataLoader collects the keys requested in one tick and issues a single batched query."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/api-and-sdk-integration","sources":["Official docs: httpx","Official docs: OpenAI Python SDK","Official docs: Anthropic Python SDK","Nygard, 2007 — Release It!"],"why":"A model provider, a vector database, a CRM, a payment processor: each is a remote system that will\nreturn 429 when you are busiest, 503 during its deploys, a half-finished stream when a load balancer\nrecycles, and a slightly different JSON shape after a release you did not read about. Your code must\nplan for every one of those, and the place that plan lives is the integration layer.\n\nThe naive version is `requests.post(url, json=payload)` sprinkled through the codebase. It has no\ntimeout (the `requests` library applies none by default), so a hung provider pins a worker forever.\nIt has no retry, so a transient 503 becomes a user-visible failure. It raises raw exceptions, so\ncallers cannot tell \"retry later\" from \"your request is invalid\". Its API key is read in twelve\nplaces, so rotation is a treasure hunt. The symptom of this design is an incident where every worker\nis blocked waiting on one vendor and the dashboard shows zero errors.\n\nThe opposite naive version is \"the SDK handles it\". It partly does: official SDKs retry on 429 and\n5xx with backoff, parse streams and type the responses. But SDK defaults are generic. Automatic\nretries silently double the cost and latency of a slow outage; default timeouts in provider SDKs are\nlong enough to let one stuck call hold a request for minutes; and an SDK upgrade can change the API\nversion it speaks. You still need a layer that owns those decisions explicitly.","how":"### Raw HTTP versus SDK\nAn SDK gives you: authentication headers, a base URL, typed request and response models, retry with\nexponential backoff on retryable statuses (honouring `Retry-After`), SSE parsing into events,\nauto-pagination iterators, and matching sync and async clients. It hides: the connection pool, the\ntimeout values, which calls it considers safe to retry, and the mapping from HTTP status to exception\nclass. Read the client constructor for `timeout` and `max_retries` before shipping; those two numbers\ndefine your worst-case latency.\n\n### The adapter: one module owns the vendor\nWrap each external system in one module that exposes your own types and raises your own exceptions.\nThis exists *because* vendor types and errors leak otherwise, and swapping or adding a provider becomes\na rewrite. Inside the adapter, classify failures into a taxonomy:\n\n- **Retryable:** 429, 502/503/504, connection errors, timeouts, truncated streams.\n- **Terminal:** 400/422 (your request is wrong), 401/403 (credentials), 404, content-policy refusals.\n- **Ambiguous:** a timeout on a non-idempotent call; the request may have completed.\n\n### Timeouts\nSet connect, read and total timeouts separately. For model calls, the read timeout governs\ntime-to-first-token on a stream and the whole response otherwise; a streaming call may legitimately\nrun for minutes while each chunk arrives within a second, so budget per-chunk rather than per-call.\nTie the total to what the user-facing request can afford, not to what the provider allows.\n\n### Retries, idempotency and budgets\nRetry retryable errors with exponential backoff and full jitter (`base × 2^n + random`), capped, and\nhonour `Retry-After` when present, *because* synchronized retries from many clients create a thundering\nherd. Retry only operations that are idempotent or carry an idempotency key; an LLM completion is\nusually safe to re-run (it costs money, not correctness), while a tool call that sends an email is\nnot. Bound retries per request and per time window so an outage does not triple your traffic.\n\n### Circuit breakers, fallbacks and connection reuse\nAfter N consecutive failures stop calling the vendor for a cool-down and fail fast or route to a\nfallback model; this protects your own latency and the vendor's recovery. Keep one client per process\nso TLS handshakes and connections are reused; creating a client per request is a common source of\nsocket exhaustion.\n\n### Configuration, observability and tests\nKeys come from a secret store or environment, never source; log the vendor's request id, status,\nlatency, attempt count and token usage on every call with a correlation id. Pin SDK versions and any\nAPI-version header. Test with recorded responses for happy paths and injected 429/timeout/truncation\nfor the failure paths, and run a small contract test against the live sandbox on a schedule.","example":"A minimal adapter around a model endpoint using `httpx`: explicit per-phase timeouts, a shared client,\nan error taxonomy, bounded retries with jitter that honour `Retry-After`, and an idempotency key so a\nretried `POST` cannot double-execute.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, os, random\nimport httpx\n\nclass RetryableError(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, msg: str, retry_after: float | None = None):\n        super().__init__(msg); self.retry_after = retry_after\n\nclass TerminalError(Exception): ...\n\nTIMEOUT = httpx.Timeout(connect=5.0, read=60.0, write=10.0, pool=5.0)   # explicit, per phase\nclient = httpx.AsyncClient(base_url=\"https://api.example-llm.com\", timeout=TIMEOUT,\n                           headers={\"Authorization\": \"Bearer \" + os.environ[\"LLM_API_KEY\"]})\n\ndef classify(r: httpx.Response) -> None:\n    if r.status_code < 400:\n        return\n    if r.status_code == 429 or r.status_code >= 500:\n        ra = r.headers.get(\"retry-after\")\n        raise RetryableError(f\"HTTP {r.status_code}\", float(ra) if ra and ra.isdigit() else None)\n    raise TerminalError(f\"HTTP {r.status_code}: {r.text[:200]}\")   # 400/401/403/422: retrying won't help\n\nasync def complete(payload: dict, idem_key: str, attempts: int = 4) -> dict:\n    for n in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            r = await client.post(\"/v1/complete\", json=payload,\n                                  headers={\"Idempotency-Key\": idem_key})   # safe to retry\n            classify(r)\n            return r.json()\n        except (RetryableError, httpx.TimeoutException, httpx.TransportError) as e:\n            if n == attempts - 1:\n                raise\n            hinted = getattr(e, \"retry_after\", None)\n            delay = hinted if hinted else min(30.0, 2 ** n + random.uniform(0, 1))  # backoff + jitter\n            await asyncio.sleep(delay)\n    raise RuntimeError(\"unreachable\")\n```","tradeoffs":["An SDK saves weeks of client code and tracks API changes for you, but it chooses retry and timeout defaults you must override, and it couples you to its release cadence; raw HTTP flips to the better choice only for tiny surfaces or unsupported languages.","Automatic retries hide transient failures from users, but each retry costs money and latency and can amplify an outage; the balance flips when the operation is non-idempotent or the budget is tight.","A thin adapter per vendor adds a layer of types and mapping, but it makes provider swaps, fallbacks and tests cheap; for a single-vendor prototype it can feel like ceremony.","Aggressive timeouts protect your latency budget, but they turn slow successes into failures and create ambiguous outcomes on non-idempotent calls."],"pitfalls":["No timeout or an inherited multi-minute default; the symptom is worker pools draining during a vendor incident while error rates stay at zero.","Retrying everything, including 400 and 401; the symptom is the same invalid request hammering the vendor four times and a rate-limit ban on top of the original bug.","Retrying a non-idempotent `POST` after a timeout without an idempotency key; the symptom is duplicate records, double charges or two emails sent.","A new client per request; the symptom is a TLS handshake on every call and \"too many open files\" under load.","Parsing vendor error bodies ad hoc in callers; the symptom is a schema change in the error payload breaking twelve call sites at once."],"when":"Every external call deserves the adapter treatment, scaled to its blast radius: a model provider on\nthe request path gets timeouts, retries, idempotency keys, a breaker and metrics; a nightly report\nAPI can live with a timeout and a retry. Use the official SDK when one exists and you have read its\ntimeout and retry settings; drop to `httpx` when the SDK lacks a feature, blocks the event loop, or\nyou need exact control over streaming. Measure attempt counts, p99 latency including retries, and\nvendor request ids in logs. In interviews, \"how do you call a third-party API in production\" is a\nstandard question; the expected answer walks timeouts, retry policy, idempotency, error taxonomy and\nobservability in that order.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds and tests the adapter: explicit timeouts, retry with jitter, idempotency keys, one shared client, and error mapping that callers can branch on.","architect":"Sets the integration standard (which errors retry, the retry budget, breaker thresholds, fallback routing) and decides where provider abstraction lives.","fde":"Integrates the AI service with a customer's internal APIs whose SDKs may be absent or unreliable, and negotiates sandbox access, rate limits and credentials for them."},"check":{"q":"Your service calls a model provider through its official SDK with defaults. During a provider\nincident, user requests take four minutes to fail and your bill for the hour triples. Explain what\nhappened and how you would configure the integration differently.","a":"The SDK retried each failing call several times with backoff, and its default timeout let each\nattempt wait minutes, so one user request became multiple long attempts; the retries also resent\nfull prompts, which is the cost spike. I would set an explicit total timeout tied to the user-facing\nbudget, cap retries to one or two with jitter and honour `Retry-After`, add a circuit breaker that\nfails fast or routes to a fallback model after consecutive failures, and emit attempt-count and\nlatency metrics so the behaviour is visible. Idempotency keys would make the remaining retries safe\nif the call had side effects."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which failure should an integration layer not retry automatically?","options":["A 503 from the provider's load balancer","A connect timeout","A 422 saying a required field is missing","A 429 with a `Retry-After` header"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retries help only when the next attempt can succeed. A validation error is deterministic, so retrying repeats the same bad request, wastes quota and delays the real fix."},{"q":"Why add an `Idempotency-Key` header when retrying a `POST` after a timeout?","options":["It makes the request faster on the second attempt","The first attempt may have completed, and the key lets the server return that result instead of executing again","It tells the server to skip authentication on retries","It disables the server's rate limiter for the retry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A timeout is ambiguous: the server may have done the work. An idempotency key lets it recognise the repeat and return the stored outcome, preventing duplicate side effects."},{"q":"What is the main reason to keep one HTTP client per process rather than per request?","options":["Per-request clients cannot send custom headers","A single client guarantees ordered delivery of requests","Connection and TLS session reuse, plus one place to configure timeouts and limits","Multiple clients would share the same API key incorrectly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each new client opens fresh connections and TLS handshakes, which cost latency and sockets. A shared client pools connections and centralises the configuration the whole service depends on."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/api-versioning-and-backward-compatibility","sources":["IETF RFC 8594 — The Sunset HTTP Header Field","Official docs: Stripe API versioning","Official docs: Anthropic API versions","Wright, 2012 — Hyrum's Law"],"why":"The clients of an API are distributed in time as well as space: a mobile app that users never update,\na partner integration built by a contractor who left, an SDK pinned in a lockfile, an agent whose\nsystem prompt describes your tool's arguments. Rename a field and each of them breaks silently at its\nown pace, and you cannot roll them back. Hyrum's law states the underlying fact: with enough users,\nevery observable behaviour of your interface becomes something somebody depends on, documented or not.\n\nThe first naive response is \"never break anything\", which freezes bad decisions forever and bloats\nthe API with fields kept only for one client. The second is \"bump to `/v2` whenever we change\nsomething\", which produces version sprawl: each version is a fork to maintain, test and secure, and\nclients never migrate because nothing forces them. The third is \"be careful\", which fails the first\ntime a well-meaning engineer makes a required field optional in the response and a strict client\nstarts throwing.\n\nThe working discipline sits between these: make additive, compatible changes the normal path so that\nversions are rare; when a break is unavoidable, version deliberately, announce a deprecation with\ndates, measure who is still on the old version, and remove it on schedule. For AI services the\ndiscipline matters twice over, because a model or prompt change can alter outputs for the same input,\nwhich is also a breaking change from the client's point of view.","how":"### What counts as breaking\nBreaking for clients: removing or renaming a field or endpoint; changing a field's type or format\n(integer to string, seconds to milliseconds); making an optional request field required; tightening\nvalidation; changing a default; changing the meaning of a value; changing status codes or the error\nenvelope; changing authentication. Usually compatible: adding an optional request field, adding a\nresponse field, adding an endpoint, relaxing validation. Contested: adding an enum value, which breaks\nclients that switch exhaustively, so document that enums are open and clients must handle unknowns.\n\n### Tolerant readers\nCompatibility is a two-sided contract. Clients should ignore unknown response fields and treat enums\nas open; servers should accept missing optional fields and ignore unknown request fields unless the\nendpoint is security-sensitive. Pydantic's default of ignoring extra fields is the tolerant choice;\n`extra=\"forbid\"` is stricter and turns a client's harmless new field into a 422, so choose it\nknowingly. This is Postel's principle applied with judgement, *because* total leniency hides bugs.\n\n### Versioning strategies\n- **URL path** (`/v1/...`, as OpenAI does): visible, routable, easy to test and cache; coarse, since\n  the whole API moves together.\n- **Header** (`Accept: application/vnd.acme.v2+json`, or a custom header such as Anthropic's\n  `anthropic-version` date): keeps URLs stable and allows per-request choice; harder to try in a\n  browser and easy to forget in a proxy.\n- **Pinned date per account** (Stripe's approach): each account is pinned to the version current\n  when it signed up; the server applies a chain of transformations to render new behaviour in old\n  shapes. Maximum compatibility, maximum server-side machinery.\n- **Query parameter** (`?version=2`): simple but mixes with filters and cache keys.\n- **Evolution only** (GraphQL's stance): no versions; add fields, mark old ones `@deprecated`, remove\n  after usage reaches zero.\n\nWhichever you choose, keep one implementation and render versions at the edge as representations,\n*because* two forked code paths drift and the old one stops getting fixes.\n\n### Deprecation lifecycle\nAnnounce in docs and changelog; emit `Sunset` (RFC 8594, an HTTP date) and a `Link` to the migration\nguide on old-version responses, and mark the route deprecated in OpenAPI; a standardized `Deprecation`\nheader also exists, so check its current value format before emitting it. Measure requests per\nversion per client so you know who is left. Run brownouts (short planned outages of the old version)\nbefore removal. Remove on the announced date.\n\n### Data and schema changes: expand and contract\nRename a column or field in three steps: add the new one, write both and read either, migrate\nconsumers, then remove the old. The same pattern applies to message schemas and tool arguments.\n\n### AI-specific contracts\nModel providers retire model versions; pin exact snapshot names and track deprecation notices. A\nprompt revision that changes output format is a contract change for everything downstream, so version\nprompts alongside code. A tool schema change can break an agent that learned the old arguments from\nits prompt or few-shot examples. Re-embedding with a new model invalidates an index built with the old\none, which is versioning applied to data.","example":"Two versioned routers in FastAPI sharing one implementation. The v2 shape renamed `summary` to\n`text` and added citations, a breaking change, so v1 is kept as a thin representation with a `Sunset`\nheader and an OpenAPI deprecation flag rather than as a forked code path.\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import APIRouter, FastAPI, Response\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict\n\nclass SummaryV2(BaseModel):             # current shape: renamed field, structured citations\n    id: str\n    text: str\n    citations: list[dict]\n    model: str\n\nclass SummaryV1(BaseModel):             # frozen shape old clients depend on\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"ignore\")   # tolerant: unknown fields are dropped, not errors\n    id: str\n    summary: str                        # renamed to `text` in v2, hence the shim below\n\nasync def summarize(doc_id: str) -> SummaryV2:\n    return SummaryV2(id=\"sum_1\", text=\"...\", citations=[{\"doc\": doc_id, \"span\": [0, 40]}],\n                     model=\"summarizer-2026-03-01\")   # pinned model snapshot is part of the contract\n\nv1, v2 = APIRouter(prefix=\"/v1\"), APIRouter(prefix=\"/v2\")\n\n@v2.post(\"/summaries\", response_model=SummaryV2)\nasync def create_v2(doc_id: str):\n    return await summarize(doc_id)\n\n@v1.post(\"/summaries\", response_model=SummaryV1, deprecated=True)   # flagged in OpenAPI docs\nasync def create_v1(doc_id: str, response: Response):\n    s = await summarize(doc_id)                      # one implementation, two representations\n    response.headers[\"Sunset\"] = \"Sat, 31 Oct 2026 00:00:00 GMT\"\n    response.headers[\"Link\"] = '</docs/migrate-v2>; rel=\"deprecation\"'\n    return SummaryV1(id=s.id, summary=s.text)        # render the old shape; drop fields v1 never promised\n\napp = FastAPI()\napp.include_router(v1)\napp.include_router(v2)\n```","tradeoffs":["Additive-only evolution keeps every client working with no migration, but the API accumulates legacy fields and awkward names; the balance flips to a versioned break when the old shape actively misleads new integrators.","Path versioning is simple and visible, but it moves the whole API at once; header or date pinning allows fine-grained evolution at the cost of transformation code and harder debugging.","Stripe-style per-account pinning gives clients indefinite stability, but every change adds a transformation layer the server must maintain and test forever.","Strict request validation (`extra=\"forbid\"`) catches client typos early, but it turns harmless additive changes by clients into outages; loosen it on public endpoints, keep it on internal ones."],"pitfalls":["Shipping a field rename as a \"small cleanup\"; the symptom is a partner integration failing days later with `KeyError` on a field you removed.","Forking the codebase per version; the symptom is a bug fixed in v2 that still ships in v1 and a security patch applied to only one of them.","Announcing a deprecation without measuring usage; the symptom is removing v1 on the date and discovering the largest customer was still on it.","Treating model or prompt updates as invisible; the symptom is downstream parsers breaking because the new model stopped emitting the code fence the old one always produced.","Clients that switch exhaustively on an enum; the symptom is a crash the day a new status value is added, even though no field changed."],"when":"Apply the compatibility rules to every change on a published interface: review each diff of a\nresponse or request model for the breaking list above, and treat prompt and model changes with the\nsame review. Introduce a version only when a break is unavoidable, and then introduce exactly one.\nMeasure requests per version per client, error rates after a release, and time-to-migrate for\ndeprecated versions. In day-to-day work this shows up as contract tests in CI that fail on breaking\nschema diffs and as release notes written for integrators. Interviews probe whether you can list\nwhat breaks, pick a strategy with reasons, and describe a deprecation lifecycle with dates and\ntelemetry rather than hope.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps changes additive, renders old versions as representations over one implementation, and wires contract tests that fail on breaking OpenAPI diffs.","architect":"Chooses the versioning strategy and deprecation policy, decides what is a published contract versus internal, and extends the policy to models, prompts and tool schemas.","fde":"Shields customer integrations from provider model deprecations and your own API changes by pinning versions and planning migrations with the customer's release calendar."},"check":{"q":"A product team wants to change the `confidence` field in your extraction API from a 0–100\ninteger to a 0–1 float, and to add a `reasons` array. How do you ship this without breaking the three\nexternal integrators on the endpoint?","a":"The `reasons` array is additive and ships immediately. The `confidence` change is breaking:\nsame name, different type and scale, so a client that thresholds at 80 would silently treat every\nresult as low confidence. Options: add a new field (`confidence_score` as a float), keep `confidence`\nas the integer, and deprecate it with a `Sunset` header and docs, removing it after telemetry shows\nzero use; or, if the API is already versioned, make the change in the next version and render the old\nshape from one implementation. I would avoid a bare `/v2` for one field. Either way, contract tests\nin CI should fail on the type change so it cannot ship by accident."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which change is backward compatible for a typical JSON API client?","options":["Renaming `summary` to `text` in the response","Adding an optional `citations` array to the response","Changing `created_at` from seconds to milliseconds","Making the optional `language` request field required"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tolerant clients ignore fields they do not know, so additions are safe. Renames, unit changes and newly required inputs all change what existing clients send or read."},{"q":"Why does Stripe-style per-account date pinning cost more on the server than path versioning?","options":["Dates are harder to parse than path segments","The server must maintain a chain of transformations that renders current behaviour in every older pinned shape","Pinned accounts cannot use the SDKs","It requires a separate database per version"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each pinned version is a representation the server must keep producing from the current implementation, so every change adds a transformation to maintain and test indefinitely."},{"q":"What should happen before an old API version is removed?","options":["Nothing; the new version has been available for a month","Return 500 from the old version so clients notice","Announce a sunset date, emit deprecation headers, measure remaining usage per client, and run brownouts","Silently redirect old-version requests to the new one"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Removal is safe only when you know who is left and they have been warned in-band and out-of-band. Brownouts surface stragglers before the hard cutoff; silent redirects hide breaking shape changes."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/database-design","sources":["Codd, 1970 — A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: PostgreSQL"],"why":"Code is rewritten every year; the data it produced is still there a decade later. If the storage layout is\nwhatever the first endpoint happened to need, three things break quickly. First, the same fact ends up\nstored in several rows (a user's email copied into every order), and the copies disagree after the first\nupdate that misses one. That is an *update anomaly*, and no amount of application discipline fixes it,\nbecause the next engineer does not know the copies exist. Second, invariants that live only in code\n(\"a message always belongs to exactly one conversation\") get violated by the one script, migration or\nmanual fix that bypasses the code, leaving orphans that crash reads months later. Third, queries that were\nfast at ten thousand rows become table scans at ten million, and by then the data layout is load-bearing\nfor a dozen services.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"store JSON blobs and figure it out later\", postpones all three problems to the\nmoment the data is largest and the original authors are gone. Database design is the practice of deciding\nup front which facts exist, which entity owns each one, which rules the database itself must enforce, and\nwhich access paths need to be cheap. For AI systems this matters more, not less: conversation history,\ntool-call logs, embeddings and evaluation results are the product's memory, and they are queried by\nhumans, dashboards and retrieval code long after the request that wrote them.","how":"### Entities, keys and relationships\nStart by naming the nouns the business reasons about (tenant, user, conversation, message, tool call, run,\ndocument, chunk) and the relationships between them with their cardinality: a conversation has many\nmessages, a message has zero or more tool calls, a document has many chunks. Each entity gets a table\nand a primary key. A **surrogate key** (bigint identity or UUID) is the default *because* natural keys\n(email, external IDs) change, get reused, or turn out not to be unique. If you use UUIDs, prefer a\ntime-ordered variant (UUIDv7) over random UUIDv4 for primary keys, because a B-tree index on random keys\ninserts into random pages, which defeats cache locality and bloats the index as the table grows.\nRelationships become foreign keys, and the database enforces them so an orphan cannot exist even when\na script bypasses the application.\n\n### Normalization: one fact, one place\nNormal forms are rules for removing redundancy, and they follow from one idea: a table should describe\none kind of thing, and every non-key column should depend on the whole key and nothing else. In practice:\n\n1. **1NF:** no repeating groups or lists packed into a column; `tags = \"a,b,c\"` becomes a `tags` table or\n   an array column with proper indexing.\n2. **2NF/3NF:** a column that depends on something other than the primary key moves to its own table. If\n   `messages` stores `user_email`, and email depends on `user_id` rather than on the message, it belongs\n   in `users`. Otherwise changing an email means rewriting every message row.\n\nNormalization is the default *because* writes are where consistency is decided; a normalized schema makes\nthe correct write the only possible write. The cost is joins on read, which is where denormalization comes\nin: a `conversations.last_message_at` column duplicated from `messages` makes the inbox query cheap, but\nnow it must be maintained in the same transaction as the insert. Denormalize when a specific query is\nmeasured to be slow, and write down the invariant you are now responsible for.\n\n### Constraints are invariants the database guarantees\n`NOT NULL`, `UNIQUE`, `CHECK (tokens_used >= 0)`, foreign keys with explicit `ON DELETE` behaviour, and\nenum-like `CHECK (role IN ('user','assistant','tool'))` are the cheapest tests you will ever write: they\nrun on every write path, including the ones you forgot. A partial unique index (`UNIQUE (tenant_id,\nexternal_id) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`) encodes \"unique among live rows\", which code alone gets wrong.\n\n### Types carry meaning\nUse `timestamptz`, never bare `timestamp`, because a timestamp without a zone is a different instant on\nevery server. Use `numeric` for money and token prices, never `float`, because binary floats cannot\nrepresent 0.1 exactly and the rounding errors accumulate in invoices. Use `jsonb` for genuinely\nsemi-structured payloads (raw model responses, tool arguments), and pull out any field you filter or join\non into a real column with a real index. Store a `schema_version` next to any jsonb you will parse later.\n\n### Multi-tenancy and deletion from day one\nEvery tenant-owned row carries `tenant_id`, every index that serves a tenant-scoped query leads with it,\nand every query filters on it; PostgreSQL row-level security can enforce the filter when you do not trust\nevery code path. Decide how deletion works before the first user asks for it: a `deleted_at` soft-delete\ncolumn keeps audit history but every query must exclude it; hard deletes with `ON DELETE CASCADE` are\nsimpler but irreversible. Retention and right-to-erasure requests are much easier when data is owned by\none clear parent row.\n\n### Indexes follow queries\nAn index is a sorted copy of some columns that makes one access path cheap and every write slightly more\nexpensive. The design question is never \"which columns are important\" but \"which `WHERE`/`ORDER BY`\npatterns run often\". A composite index on `(conversation_id, created_at)` serves \"messages of this\nconversation in order\" in one range scan; the same two columns in the other order do not. Equality\ncolumns go first, the range or sort column last. Foreign-key columns usually need an index because joins\nand cascading deletes look rows up by them.","example":"A minimal schema for an LLM application. Note the tenant column on every row, the constraints that encode\nrules, the jsonb for raw payloads with an indexed extracted field, and the one composite index built for\nthe one hot query (load a conversation's messages in order).\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE conversations (\n  id           uuid PRIMARY KEY,\n  tenant_id    uuid NOT NULL,\n  user_id      uuid NOT NULL,\n  title        text,\n  created_at   timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),\n  deleted_at   timestamptz\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE messages (\n  id              bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  tenant_id       uuid NOT NULL,\n  conversation_id uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES conversations(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  role            text NOT NULL CHECK (role IN ('system','user','assistant','tool')),\n  content         text NOT NULL,\n  model           text,\n  input_tokens    integer CHECK (input_tokens >= 0),\n  output_tokens   integer CHECK (output_tokens >= 0),\n  raw_response    jsonb,                     -- provider payload, never filtered on directly\n  schema_version  smallint NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n  created_at      timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\n-- the hot path: \"messages of one conversation, oldest first\"\nCREATE INDEX messages_by_conversation ON messages (conversation_id, created_at);\n-- the tenant-scoped dashboard query: spend per tenant per day\nCREATE INDEX messages_by_tenant_time ON messages (tenant_id, created_at);\n\nCREATE TABLE tool_calls (\n  id            bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  message_id    bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES messages(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  tool_name     text NOT NULL,\n  arguments     jsonb NOT NULL,\n  status        text NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('pending','succeeded','failed')),\n  idempotency_key text,\n  UNIQUE (idempotency_key)                   -- a retried call cannot run twice\n);\n```","tradeoffs":["Normalization guarantees one fact in one place at the cost of joins; it flips toward denormalization only when a specific read path is measured to be slow and its duplicate can be maintained transactionally.","Surrogate keys are stable and cheap to join, but you must still add a unique constraint on the natural identifier or duplicates will silently appear.","UUID keys can be generated client-side and merged across systems, but random UUIDs cost index locality and 16 bytes per reference versus 8 for bigint; time-ordered UUIDs recover most of the locality.","`jsonb` absorbs schema changes without migrations, but every field you later filter on without a column and index becomes a sequential scan, and nothing enforces its shape.","Every index speeds one read pattern and taxes every insert and update; on write-heavy tables such as event logs, each extra index is a measurable throughput cost.","Foreign keys and `CHECK`s cost a lookup per write but catch bugs at the only point that sees every path; dropping them \"for speed\" trades a few microseconds for data you can no longer trust."],"pitfalls":["Omitting `tenant_id` from child tables and relying on joins to the parent for scoping; the symptom is a query that returns another tenant's rows the first time someone forgets the join.","Using `float` for money or token prices; the symptom is invoices that are off by a cent and tests that compare with tolerances.","Bare `timestamp` columns; the symptom is events that shift by hours after a server or container changes time zone, and \"yesterday\" reports that disagree.","Indexing columns rather than queries; the symptom is ten single-column indexes, slow writes, and the one hot query still doing a sort because no composite index matches its `WHERE` and `ORDER BY`.","Entity-attribute-value tables (\"one table of key/value pairs for everything\") to avoid migrations; the symptom is five-way self-joins to reconstruct one object and no constraints on anything.","Storing multi-megabyte payloads (full prompts, document text) in the hottest table; the symptom is bloated tables, slow vacuums and a cache that no longer holds the working set.","Designing deletion last; the symptom is a right-to-erasure request that takes a week because data is scattered across tables with no ownership chain."],"when":"Reach for deliberate design whenever data will be read by anything other than the code that wrote it:\ndashboards, evaluations, audits, other services, or future you. For a throwaway prototype, a single jsonb\ncolumn is fine; the moment a second query pattern appears, extract real columns. Measure before\ndenormalizing: look at `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` on the slow query and at the write rate of the table you would\nduplicate into. In day-to-day work this shows up as migration reviews (\"why is there no index for the\nquery this endpoint runs?\", \"what happens to messages when a conversation is deleted?\"). In interviews,\nexpect to be handed a product description and asked to draw tables, keys and indexes, then defend each\nconstraint and explain which query each index serves.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the migration, picks types and constraints, and adds the composite index that matches the query the new endpoint runs, verified with `EXPLAIN`.","architect":"Owns the ownership model: which service owns which tables, how tenancy is enforced, the retention and deletion policy, and when a read model may be denormalized.","fde":"Maps the customer's existing schema and identifiers onto the product's model, finds the missing tenant or ownership keys, and plans how their history will be imported and later erased."},"check":{"q":"A chat product stores each conversation as one jsonb document containing all its messages. Reads\nare fast today. What breaks as it grows, and how would you restructure it?","a":"Appending a message rewrites the whole document, so write cost grows with conversation length and\nlong conversations create bloat and lock contention. You cannot index or constrain individual messages,\nso \"all messages by this tool across tenants\" or \"token spend per day\" become full scans that parse JSON.\nDeleting one message or one user's data means rewriting documents. The fix is a `messages` table with\n`tenant_id`, `conversation_id`, `role`, token counts and `created_at`, a composite index on\n`(conversation_id, created_at)` for the conversation view, and a second on `(tenant_id, created_at)` for\nreporting; keep the raw provider response in a jsonb column on the message row, where it is not filtered\non. If the inbox view needs `last_message_at`, denormalize that single column and maintain it in the same\ntransaction as the insert."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a composite index on (conversation_id, created_at) better than two single-column indexes for loading a conversation's messages in order?","options":["Composite indexes are stored in memory while single-column indexes are on disk","One range scan over the composite index returns the rows already in the right order, while separate indexes require combining results and then sorting","Single-column indexes cannot be used together in the same query","The composite index takes less space than two single-column indexes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The planner can walk the composite index from the first matching conversation_id in created_at order, so no sort step is needed. Two single-column indexes can be combined with a bitmap scan but still require a separate sort."},{"q":"A team drops foreign keys \"to make inserts faster\". Which failure is this most likely to cause?","options":["Queries become slower because the planner can no longer use joins","The database refuses to create indexes on the former foreign-key columns","Orphan rows appear whenever any script or bug deletes a parent without its children, and reads fail later","Transactions can no longer be rolled back on those tables"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A foreign key is checked on every write path, including the ones the application code does not cover. Without it, orphans accumulate silently and surface as missing-join bugs or crashes much later."},{"q":"When is denormalizing a column such as conversations.last_message_at the right call?","options":["Whenever a join would otherwise be required, because joins are always expensive","Never, because it violates third normal form","When a specific frequent query is measured to be slow and the duplicate can be updated in the same transaction as the source row","Only when the database does not support indexes on the child table"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Denormalization buys read speed by taking on a consistency obligation. It is justified by measurement and made safe by maintaining the copy transactionally; doing it by default reintroduces update anomalies."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/sql","sources":["Codd, 1970 — A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks","Official docs: PostgreSQL","Winand — Use The Index, Luke"],"why":"Before the relational model, databases were navigational: a program walked pointers from record to record,\nso every program encoded the physical layout, and changing the layout meant changing every program. Codd's\ninsight was to describe data as relations (tables of tuples) and queries as algebra over them, so the\n*what* could be stated independently of the *how*. SQL is the practical language that came out of that,\nand the planner is the component that turns the what into a how at run time, using statistics about the\nactual data.\n\nThe naive alternative in application code is the loop: fetch the conversations, then for each one fetch\nits messages, then for each message count tokens. That is the N+1 pattern, and it fails for two reasons.\nEach round trip to the database costs a network hop (hundreds of microseconds to milliseconds), so 1,000\nconversations cost 1,001 queries and seconds of wall time. And the database never sees the whole\nquestion, so it cannot pick a hash join or use an index to do the work in one pass. A single query with a\njoin and an aggregate lets the engine do it in one scan.\n\nAI backends are heavy SQL users even when the model is the star: per-tenant token spend, latency\npercentiles per model, the last N messages of a conversation, eval results joined to prompt versions, and\nhybrid retrieval that mixes a vector similarity with a metadata filter are all one query each when\nwritten well and a slow fan-out of calls when written badly.","how":"### Logical evaluation order\nA `SELECT` statement is written in one order and evaluated in another. The logical order is:\n`FROM` and `JOIN` (build the row set), `WHERE` (filter rows), `GROUP BY` (collapse into groups),\n`HAVING` (filter groups), `SELECT` (compute output columns, including window functions), `DISTINCT`,\n`ORDER BY`, then `LIMIT`/`OFFSET`. This explains the classic confusions: you cannot reference a `SELECT`\nalias in `WHERE` because `WHERE` runs first; `HAVING` exists because aggregates do not exist yet when\n`WHERE` runs; window functions see the filtered, grouped rows because they run during `SELECT`.\n\n### Joins are set operations, not loops\nAn inner join keeps rows that match on both sides; a left join keeps every left row and fills the right\nside with NULLs when nothing matches, which is how you find conversations with no messages\n(`WHERE m.id IS NULL`). `EXISTS`/`NOT EXISTS` express \"has at least one\" without duplicating rows the way\na join would. The engine picks the physical algorithm: a **nested loop** when one side is tiny or has an\nindex on the join key, a **hash join** when both sides are large and unsorted, a **merge join** when both\nsides are already sorted on the key. You do not pick these; you make them possible with indexes and\naccurate statistics.\n\n### Aggregates and window functions\n`GROUP BY` collapses rows; a window function (`SUM(...) OVER (PARTITION BY tenant_id ORDER BY day)`)\ncomputes an aggregate *per row* without collapsing, which is how you get running totals, rankings and\n\"latest row per group\" in a single pass. PostgreSQL's `DISTINCT ON (conversation_id) ... ORDER BY\nconversation_id, created_at DESC` is the idiomatic \"newest message per conversation\".\n\n### How the planner decides\n`EXPLAIN ANALYZE` shows the tree the planner chose and the real row counts and timings. The planner\nestimates cost from table statistics (row counts, value distributions gathered by `ANALYZE`) and chooses\nbetween a sequential scan (read everything; cheapest when most rows qualify), an index scan (walk a B-tree\nto the matching rows; cheapest when few qualify) and a bitmap scan (collect matching pages from one or\nmore indexes, then read them in physical order). A predicate is **sargable** (can use an index) when the\nindexed column appears bare on one side of a comparison: `created_at >= $1` can use an index;\n`date(created_at) = $1` cannot, because the index stores `created_at`, not its date. `LIKE 'abc%'` can use\na B-tree; `LIKE '%abc%'` cannot. The planner is only as good as its statistics, so a table that grew 100×\nsince the last `ANALYZE` gets bad plans.\n\n### Parameters, not string formatting\nQueries take parameters (`$1`, `%s`, `?` depending on the driver) that are sent separately from the SQL\ntext. This is the only reliable defence against SQL injection *because* the database never parses user\ninput as SQL; it also lets the server reuse a prepared plan. String interpolation of user input into a\nquery is a vulnerability regardless of how well you escape.\n\n### Pagination and the OFFSET trap\n`LIMIT 20 OFFSET 100000` still reads and discards 100,000 rows; cost grows linearly with page depth.\nKeyset pagination (`WHERE (created_at, id) < ($last_ts, $last_id) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC\nLIMIT 20`) seeks directly to the next page using the index and costs the same on page 1 and page 5,000.\n\n### Where the ORM fits\nORMs generate SQL from objects, which is convenient for CRUD and dangerous for reporting: lazy-loaded\nrelationships are the usual source of N+1, and complex aggregations are often clearer as SQL. The\nworking rule is to let the ORM handle simple writes and reads by key, and to write (and `EXPLAIN`) the\nhot or analytical queries by hand.","example":"Two queries that an LLM backend runs constantly. The first is a per-tenant spend report with a running\ntotal, done in one pass with a window function. The second is \"newest message per conversation\" for an\ninbox view using `DISTINCT ON`, plus keyset pagination so page depth does not change the cost.\n\n```sql\n-- 1. Daily token spend per tenant for the last 7 days, with a running total.\n--    Uses the (tenant_id, created_at) index: a range scan, then one hash aggregate.\nSELECT tenant_id,\n       date_trunc('day', created_at) AS day,\n       SUM(input_tokens + output_tokens)                                 AS tokens,\n       SUM(SUM(input_tokens + output_tokens))\n         OVER (PARTITION BY tenant_id ORDER BY date_trunc('day', created_at)) AS running_tokens\nFROM   messages\nWHERE  created_at >= now() - interval '7 days'      -- sargable: bare column vs constant\n  AND  role = 'assistant'\nGROUP  BY tenant_id, date_trunc('day', created_at)\nORDER  BY tenant_id, day;\n\n-- 2. Inbox: the newest message of each conversation for one user, 20 at a time.\n--    Keyset pagination: pass the last row's (created_at, id) back instead of an OFFSET.\nSELECT c.id, c.title, m.content, m.created_at\nFROM   conversations c\nJOIN   LATERAL (\n         SELECT content, created_at, id\n         FROM   messages\n         WHERE  conversation_id = c.id\n         ORDER  BY created_at DESC\n         LIMIT  1                                     -- hits (conversation_id, created_at)\n       ) m ON true\nWHERE  c.tenant_id = $1 AND c.user_id = $2 AND c.deleted_at IS NULL\n  AND  (m.created_at, m.id) < ($3, $4)                -- cursor from the previous page\nORDER  BY m.created_at DESC, m.id DESC\nLIMIT  20;\n```\n\nRun each with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` and check two things: that the scan on `messages` is an index\nscan rather than a sequential scan, and that the estimated row count is within an order of magnitude of\nthe actual one. A large mismatch means stale statistics or a predicate the planner cannot estimate.","tradeoffs":["Declarative queries let the planner adapt to data growth, but you give up control of the algorithm; when the planner guesses wrong you fix statistics or indexes, not the query's \"steps\".","One big joined query minimizes round trips but can produce a huge intermediate result; when a join multiplies rows (one conversation × many messages × many tool calls), two targeted queries are cheaper.","Indexes make predicates cheap but each one slows writes and consumes memory; a reporting query that runs hourly rarely justifies an index that every insert pays for.","ORMs speed up CRUD and keep types in sync, but they hide the SQL; the moment a query touches more than two tables or an aggregate, hand-written SQL is usually both faster and clearer.","Keyset pagination is constant-cost but cannot jump to \"page 37\"; OFFSET can, at linear cost, so pick by whether users actually jump or just scroll.","Common table expressions make queries readable; since PostgreSQL 12 the planner can inline a CTE that is referenced once, so readability no longer forces a materialization cost, but `MATERIALIZED` is there when you need the optimisation fence."],"pitfalls":["The N+1 loop hidden behind an ORM relationship; the symptom is a request that issues hundreds of identical small queries in the log, each fast, together slow.","Wrapping an indexed column in a function (`lower(email) = $1`, `date(created_at) = $1`); the symptom is a sequential scan in `EXPLAIN` despite an index existing. Use an expression index or rewrite as a range.","`NOT IN (subquery)` where the subquery can return NULL; the symptom is a query that silently returns zero rows. Use `NOT EXISTS`.","`SELECT *` in application code; the symptom is a column added for a migration that doubles network payload and breaks positional unpacking in a client.","Deep `OFFSET` pagination; the symptom is page one in milliseconds and page two hundred in seconds, with the database CPU climbing as users scroll.","Building SQL with f-strings or template literals; the symptom is a security finding, or a query that breaks on the first title containing an apostrophe.","Trusting plans from a small dev database; the symptom is a query that was instant in staging and does a sequential scan over 50 million rows in production because the planner's choice flips with size."],"when":"SQL is the right tool whenever the question is relational: filtering, joining, aggregating or ranking\nrows across entities. Write it by hand for anything that joins more than two tables, aggregates, or runs\non a hot path, and let the ORM handle simple key lookups and inserts. Before shipping a new query, run\n`EXPLAIN ANALYZE` against production-sized data and confirm the scan types and row estimates; after\nshipping, watch `pg_stat_statements` for the queries with the highest total time, not the highest mean.\nIn interviews, expect to write a query with a join, a `GROUP BY` and a window function, explain why a\ngiven predicate cannot use an index, and diagnose an N+1 from a description of symptoms.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the spend and inbox queries, replaces ORM loops with joins, and reads `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` to confirm each hot query uses the index it was designed for.","architect":"Decides which questions are answered by the transactional database versus a warehouse or search index, and sets the rule that hot-path SQL is reviewed with its plan.","fde":"Writes the one-off queries that prove value in a customer's data, and reads their slow-query logs to find the N+1 or missing index behind a complaint about latency."},"check":{"q":"A dashboard query filtering `messages` by `tenant_id` and the last 24 hours takes 8 seconds in\nproduction but 20 ms in staging. There is an index on `(tenant_id, created_at)`. What do you check and why?","a":"First run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` in production to see whether the index is used at all. If the\nplan shows a sequential scan, the predicate is probably not sargable (a function wrapped around\n`created_at`, or a type mismatch such as comparing a `timestamptz` column to a `text` parameter that\nforces a cast) or the statistics are stale after rapid growth, so `ANALYZE messages` and compare. If the\nindex is used but the query is still slow, look at actual versus estimated rows: one huge tenant can make\nthe range scan return millions of rows, in which case the aggregate itself is the cost and a pre-aggregated\ndaily table is the fix. Staging is fast simply because it is small; the planner's choice flips with data\nvolume, which is why plans must be checked against production-sized data."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can WHERE date(created_at) = '2026-10-20' not use a B-tree index on created_at?","options":["Date comparisons are never indexable in PostgreSQL","The index stores created_at values, not the result of date(), so the planner cannot seek on the function's output","The literal must be cast to timestamptz before the index can be used","B-tree indexes only support equality on integer columns"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The index is ordered by the raw column. Applying a function hides the column from the planner; rewrite as a range (`created_at >= '2026-10-20' AND created_at < '2026-10-21'`) or create an expression index."},{"q":"An endpoint loads 500 conversations and then each conversation's last message via an ORM relationship. Which fix addresses the root cause?","options":["Add an index on messages.content to speed up each lookup","Increase the connection pool size so the 500 queries run in parallel","Replace the per-row lookups with one query that joins or uses a lateral subquery to fetch the latest message per conversation","Cache the conversation list in Redis so the loop runs less often"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cost is 501 round trips, not slow individual queries. A single joined or lateral query lets the database do the work in one pass; more connections or a cache only hides the pattern."},{"q":"What is the main advantage of keyset pagination over LIMIT/OFFSET?","options":["It returns rows in a stable order even without an ORDER BY clause","It allows jumping directly to any page number","It seeks to the next page through the index, so deep pages cost the same as the first page","It avoids the need for an index on the sort column"],"answer":2,"explanation":"OFFSET reads and discards every preceding row, so cost grows with page depth. A keyset cursor uses the index to start exactly where the previous page ended; the trade is losing random page jumps."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/nosql","sources":["DeCandia et al., 2007 — Dynamo: Amazon's Highly Available Key-value Store","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Amazon DynamoDB","Official docs: MongoDB"],"why":"A relational database on one machine answers any question you can write in SQL, but it has a ceiling:\none machine's disk, memory and write throughput. Past that you must partition, and the moment rows live\non different machines, a join between them becomes a network operation and a transaction across them\nbecomes a distributed commit. Both are possible, but they are slow and fragile, and they are exactly the\noperations the relational model makes casual. Early web-scale systems hit this wall and responded by\nrestricting the data model: if every operation touches one partition, identified by a key, then each\nmachine can serve its partition independently, nodes can be added without coordination, and a machine\nfailure affects only its share of keys.\n\nThe naive alternative, sharding a relational database by hand, pushes the join and transaction problem\ninto application code where every engineer must know the sharding scheme, and it keeps the schema rigid\nwhile the data is spread across nodes that cannot be migrated atomically. NoSQL systems make the\nconstraint explicit instead: you declare a partition key, the store guarantees cheap operations within a\npartition, and anything across partitions is either unsupported or clearly expensive. A second\nmotivation is heterogeneous records: a document store lets items of one collection carry different\nfields, which suits event payloads and tool outputs whose shape varies by type.\n\nThe cost is real. Every query you did not design a key or index for is a full scan or impossible, and\n\"schemaless\" simply means the schema lives in your code, unenforced.","how":"### The families\n- **Key-value** (Redis, DynamoDB used simply, Riak): get and put a value by key. The simplest and the\n  fastest; no queries other than by key.\n- **Document** (MongoDB, Couchbase, DynamoDB with nested attributes): the value is a JSON-like document\n  with nested fields; you can index and query on fields inside it, within a collection.\n- **Wide-column** (Cassandra, ScyllaDB, Bigtable): rows grouped by a partition key and ordered within the\n  partition by clustering columns; built for very high write rates and time-ordered reads within a key.\n- **Graph** (Neo4j): nodes and edges, for queries that are mostly traversals (\"friends of friends\").\nSearch engines and vector databases are specialised stores that behave like document stores with a\ndifferent index at the core; they are covered in their own lessons.\n\n### Partitioning by key\nThe store hashes the partition key to pick a node (or a range of keys maps to a node). Every operation\nthat carries the key is routed to one node and costs one lookup regardless of total data size, which is\nthe whole point. Operations without the key must fan out to every node, so a design question is: \"which\nkey will every hot query have in hand?\" In DynamoDB this is the partition key plus an optional sort key;\nin Cassandra the partition key plus clustering columns; in MongoDB the shard key.\n\n### Replication and consistency\nEach partition is replicated to N nodes for durability. Writes are acknowledged after W replicas accept\nthem and reads consult R replicas; when W + R > N a read is guaranteed to overlap a recent write (quorum).\nChoosing W = 1 gives the fastest writes and the possibility that a read a moment later sees the old value:\n**eventual consistency**. Most stores let you choose per operation (Cassandra consistency levels,\nDynamoDB's `ConsistentRead`), and strongly consistent reads cost more and may fail when a replica is\ndown. This is the practical face of the CAP trade-off: during a partition, a store either refuses some\nrequests or serves possibly-stale data.\n\n### Modelling: access patterns first\nRelational design starts from entities and normalizes; NoSQL design starts from the list of queries and\nshapes the data so each one is a single-partition read. Two decisions dominate:\n1. **Embed or reference.** Embed child data inside the parent document when it is read together and\n   bounded in size (a message with its tool calls). Reference by id when the child set is unbounded or\n   shared (all messages of a conversation that may run to thousands). Documents have hard size limits\n   (16 MB in MongoDB, 400 KB per item in DynamoDB), and an unbounded embedded array will hit them.\n2. **Denormalize and duplicate.** Because there are no joins, data needed by two queries is often stored\n   twice, in two key layouts, and the application updates both. DynamoDB's single-table design takes this\n   to its conclusion: every entity type in one table with composite keys like `TENANT#t1#USER#u9` /\n   `SESSION#2026-10-20T10:00`, and global secondary indexes (GSIs) that re-key the same items for a second\n   access pattern. A GSI is maintained asynchronously, so reads through it are eventually consistent.\n\n### Hot partitions\nThroughput is provisioned per partition, not per table. If one tenant produces 40% of all writes and\ntenant id is the partition key, that partition throttles while the rest of the cluster idles. Fixes are\nto add a high-cardinality component to the key (tenant + conversation, or tenant + a shard suffix) so a\nbusy tenant spreads over several partitions, at the cost of fan-out reads for \"everything for this tenant\".\n\n### Transactions and indexes\nModern document stores support multi-document transactions, and DynamoDB offers transactional writes\nacross a few items, but they are slower, limited in scope and not the default idiom. Secondary indexes\nexist in all of them, but each one is a copy of the data maintained on every write, which is why\n\"index everything\" costs far more here than in PostgreSQL.","example":"Agent sessions and their messages in DynamoDB, designed from two access patterns: \"list a user's recent\nsessions\" and \"load the last 20 messages of a session\". Each is a single-partition `Query`. Messages are\nreferenced, not embedded, because a session can grow without bound. The sort key carries a zero-padded\nsequence so lexical order is chronological.\n\n```python\nimport boto3, time\nfrom boto3.dynamodb.conditions import Key\n\ntable = boto3.resource(\"dynamodb\").Table(\"agent-app\")\n\ndef create_session(tenant, user, session_id, title):\n    table.put_item(Item={\n        \"pk\": f\"TENANT#{tenant}#USER#{user}\",\n        \"sk\": f\"SESSION#{int(time.time()*1000):013d}#{session_id}\",\n        \"title\": title, \"session_id\": session_id,\n    })\n\ndef append_message(session_id, seq, role, content):\n    table.put_item(\n        Item={\"pk\": f\"SESSION#{session_id}\", \"sk\": f\"MSG#{seq:08d}\",\n              \"role\": role, \"content\": content},\n        ConditionExpression=\"attribute_not_exists(sk)\",   # idempotent on retry\n    )\n\ndef recent_sessions(tenant, user, limit=10):\n    r = table.query(\n        KeyConditionExpression=Key(\"pk\").eq(f\"TENANT#{tenant}#USER#{user}\")\n                               & Key(\"sk\").begins_with(\"SESSION#\"),\n        ScanIndexForward=False, Limit=limit)              # newest first\n    return r[\"Items\"]\n\ndef last_messages(session_id, limit=20):\n    r = table.query(\n        KeyConditionExpression=Key(\"pk\").eq(f\"SESSION#{session_id}\")\n                               & Key(\"sk\").begins_with(\"MSG#\"),\n        ScanIndexForward=False, Limit=limit,\n        ConsistentRead=True)                              # read-your-own-write after append\n    return list(reversed(r[\"Items\"]))\n```\n\nA query the design does not support, \"all messages mentioning refunds across all tenants\", would be a\nfull table scan; if it matters, the data also goes to a search index.","tradeoffs":["Single-partition operations scale horizontally with no ceiling in practice, but any query that lacks the partition key becomes a scatter-gather or a scan; the trade flips when ad-hoc questions dominate.","Flexible schemas let different record shapes coexist and evolve without migrations, but nothing enforces them, so validation moves into code and old shapes linger in the data forever.","Eventual consistency gives low latency and availability during failures, but a client that writes then reads may see stale data; pay for consistent reads on the paths where users would notice.","Denormalizing for read patterns makes each read one call, but every update must touch every copy, and a missed copy is a silent inconsistency with no foreign key to catch it.","Managed stores like DynamoDB remove operations work and give predictable per-request cost, but a new access pattern can mean a new index or a data migration rather than a new query.","A document store avoids the object-relational mismatch for nested, read-together data, but it loses the database's ability to join, constrain and aggregate across entities."],"pitfalls":["Choosing NoSQL \"for scale\" at tens of gigabytes; the symptom is a team rebuilding joins, aggregates and constraints in application code for data that a single PostgreSQL instance would handle easily.","Partitioning by tenant alone; the symptom is throttling errors on one partition while the cluster's overall utilisation is low.","Embedding an unbounded array (all messages inside the session document); the symptom is writes that get slower as the conversation grows, then a hard document-size error.","Expecting read-your-own-write on default eventually consistent reads; the symptom is a user who posts a message and does not see it on the next refresh, intermittently.","Issuing scans or non-key queries from request paths; the symptom is latency that rises with table size and a bill dominated by read capacity.","Letting document shapes drift (some records with `tokens`, some with `token_count`); the symptom is code full of defensive `get` calls and analytics that undercount.","Relying on secondary indexes as if they were free; the symptom is write cost multiplying with each index and GSI reads that lag the base table."],"when":"Reach for a NoSQL store when you can write down the handful of access patterns up front, when each one\ncan be keyed to a single partition, and when the write rate, data volume or availability requirement\nexceeds what one relational node provides comfortably. Typical fits in AI systems: per-session agent\nstate and message logs keyed by session, high-volume event and trace ingestion, feature or cache lookups\nby id. Stay relational while the data fits one machine and the questions are still changing; add a\nspecialised store (search, vector, key-value cache) beside PostgreSQL rather than replacing it. Measure\npartition heat and consistent-read usage once live. In interviews, expect to design keys for a given set\nof queries and to explain what happens to the query you forgot.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Designs partition and sort keys from the query list, writes conditional puts for idempotency, and chooses consistent reads only on the paths where staleness would be visible.","architect":"Decides which data leaves the relational system of record, how copies stay in sync, and what consistency each read path promises to users and downstream services.","fde":"Reads the customer's existing DynamoDB or Mongo model, spots the hot partition or the unbounded document, and plans how conversation data will be exported for evaluation and compliance."},"check":{"q":"You are storing agent runs in DynamoDB with `tenant_id` as the partition key and `run_id` as the\nsort key. One enterprise tenant generates half of all traffic. What goes wrong and how do you fix it?","a":"All of that tenant's reads and writes land on one partition, which has a fixed throughput ceiling,\nso that tenant sees throttling while the table's total capacity is mostly unused. Raising provisioned\ncapacity does not help because the limit is per partition. The fix is to spread the tenant across\npartitions by adding high-cardinality data to the key, for example `tenant_id#user_id` or\n`tenant_id#shard` where shard is derived from the run id. The cost is that \"all runs for the tenant\" now\nrequires querying each shard and merging, so you keep a GSI or a separate summary item for tenant-wide\nviews, accepting that it is eventually consistent. The general lesson is that the key must distribute\nload, not just identify ownership."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a document store make joins across collections expensive or unsupported?","options":["JSON documents cannot contain foreign keys","Data is partitioned by key across nodes, so a join would require moving matching records between machines","Document stores do not support indexes on nested fields","Joins require a fixed schema that document stores lack"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Horizontal scaling relies on each operation touching one partition. A general join must combine records that live on different nodes, which is the network-heavy operation partitioning was designed to avoid."},{"q":"A session document embeds every message and writes slow down as conversations grow. What is the correct restructuring?","options":["Compress the message array before storing it","Switch the session document to a strongly consistent write","Store messages as separate items keyed by session id with an ordered sort key, and reference them from the session","Add a secondary index on the embedded message array"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Embedding suits bounded, read-together data. An unbounded array makes every append rewrite the whole document and eventually hits the size limit; separate items keyed by session keep appends constant-cost."},{"q":"With N = 3 replicas, which setting guarantees a read sees the latest acknowledged write?","options":["W = 1, R = 1, because the write is replicated eventually","W = 1, R = 2, because two readers will outvote the stale replica","W = 2, R = 2, because the write and read replica sets must overlap","Any setting, because replication is synchronous by default"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Overlap requires W + R > N. With W = 2 and R = 2, at least one replica consulted by the read holds the write. W = 1 with R = 2 can read two replicas that both missed it."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/redis","sources":["Official docs: Redis","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"Every multi-instance backend needs some state that is shared across processes and read far more often\nthan it changes: the cached answer to an expensive call, the count of requests a client made this minute,\nwho currently holds a lock, the session for a logged-in user. Putting that in the primary database works\nuntil it does not: a relational database stores rows on disk with transaction logging and MVCC, so a\nsimple counter increment costs a write-ahead-log append and a row version, and ten thousand increments a\nsecond from a rate limiter compete with real transactions for the same CPU and I/O. Keeping it in process\nmemory is fast but wrong as soon as there are two processes, because each has its own copy and a load\nbalancer sends consecutive requests to different ones.\n\nThe naive middle ground, a plain key-value cache like memcached, solves the shared-lookup problem but\noffers only get and set, so anything that needs structure, such as \"add this item to the user's\nsorted-by-time list and trim it to 100\", becomes a read-modify-write race between two servers. Redis\nexists to make those operations single, atomic commands on rich data structures, executed in memory\nwith sub-millisecond latency, and to add just enough persistence, replication and clustering that it can\nbe trusted with more than a cache. In AI backends it ends up holding response caches keyed by prompt\nhash, per-tenant token-rate limits, semaphores that cap concurrent model calls, and the broker behind\njob queues.","how":"### Data structures, not just values\nEach key maps to one typed value: a **string** (also used for integers, with `INCR`), a **hash** (field\nto value map, good for an object), a **list** (push/pop at either end: a queue), a **set** and **sorted\nset** (members with a score, kept ordered: leaderboards, sliding windows, scheduled jobs), and a\n**stream** (an append-only log with consumer groups, the closest thing to Kafka inside Redis). Commands\noperate on the structure in place, so \"push onto the list and trim it\" is two commands rather than a\nround trip to fetch, modify and write back. Keys can carry a TTL, after which they are deleted; TTLs are\nhow caches and rate-limit windows expire without a cleanup job.\n\n### Single-threaded execution is the point\nCommands are executed one at a time on a single thread (newer versions use extra threads for network\nI/O, but command execution stays serialized). That is why every command is atomic with no locking, and\nwhy `INCR`, `SET ... NX`, `ZADD` and friends can be used as the primitives of counters, locks and\nschedulers across many application servers. It is also why one slow command (`KEYS *`, a huge `SMEMBERS`,\na long Lua script) stalls every client. For multi-command atomicity there are `MULTI`/`EXEC` transactions\nand Lua scripts, which run as a single unit on the server.\n\n### Persistence and what \"durable\" means here\nRedis is memory-first; persistence is a configuration choice. **RDB** writes periodic snapshots, cheap\nbut losing everything since the last snapshot on a crash. **AOF** appends every write to a log, and its\n`fsync` policy decides the window of loss (every write, every second, or left to the OS). Neither makes\nRedis a system of record: treat data in Redis as rebuildable, or at least as acceptable to lose a second\nof, and put anything that must survive in a database.\n\n### Memory limits and eviction\n`maxmemory` caps the dataset; when it is reached, the eviction policy decides what happens:\n`noeviction` (writes fail), `allkeys-lru` (evict least recently used anything), `volatile-lru` and\n`volatile-ttl` (evict only keys with a TTL). A cache wants `allkeys-lru`; a queue or lock store wants\n`noeviction`, because evicting a job silently is worse than failing loudly. This is why mixing a cache\nand a queue in one instance is a design error.\n\n### Replication, Sentinel and Cluster\nA primary replicates asynchronously to replicas; Sentinel monitors and promotes a replica on failure.\nBecause replication is asynchronous, a failover can lose the last writes, which matters for locks and\nqueues. Redis Cluster shards keys across nodes by hashing the key into 16,384 slots; multi-key commands\nthen only work when all keys hash to the same slot, which you control with hash tags such as\n`{tenant42}:limit`. Managed offerings (ElastiCache, Memorystore, Upstash, Redis Cloud) run these for you.\nNote that Redis changed its licence in 2024 and the community fork Valkey appeared; the commands are the\nsame, but check which one your platform ships.\n\n### The patterns that matter in AI backends\n- **Cache-aside:** look up `cache:{model}:{sha256(prompt+params)}`; on miss, call the model, `SET` with\n  a TTL. Only safe when the call is deterministic enough for your use (temperature 0, or you accept a\n  stored sample).\n- **Rate limiting:** `INCR` a per-tenant-per-window key and set its TTL on first increment, or a sorted\n  set of timestamps for a sliding window. Atomic, so it is correct across all servers.\n- **Concurrency semaphore:** cap concurrent calls to a model provider with a counter bounded by a Lua\n  script, so a burst does not trip the provider's limits.\n- **Locks:** `SET lock:key token NX PX 30000` acquires only if absent and expires if the holder dies.\n  Release by checking the token in a Lua script. Expiry means a slow holder can lose the lock while\n  still working, so protected resources need a fencing token if correctness matters.\n- **Queues and streams:** lists for simple job queues (`LPUSH`/`BRPOP`), streams with consumer groups\n  for acknowledged, replayable delivery; BullMQ, Celery and RQ build on these.","example":"Three primitives in redis-py: an LLM response cache, a fixed-window rate limiter made atomic with a Lua\nscript, and a lock with a safe release. The script runs server-side so the increment and the TTL cannot\nbe separated by a crash.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, redis, time, uuid\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\n\nr = redis.Redis(host=\"localhost\", port=6379, decode_responses=True)\n\ndef cached_completion(model, prompt, call):\n    key = \"cache:\" + model + \":\" + hashlib.sha256(prompt.encode()).hexdigest()\n    hit = r.get(key)\n    if hit is not None:\n        return json.loads(hit)\n    out = call(model, prompt)                 # the expensive provider call\n    r.set(key, json.dumps(out), ex=3600)      # 1-hour TTL; eviction handles the rest\n    return out\n\nRATE_LUA = \"\"\"\nlocal n = redis.call('INCR', KEYS[1])\nif n == 1 then redis.call('EXPIRE', KEYS[1], ARGV[1]) end\nreturn n\n\"\"\"\nrate_script = r.register_script(RATE_LUA)\n\ndef allow(tenant, limit=60, window_s=60):\n    key = f\"rl:{tenant}:{int(time.time() // window_s)}\"\n    return rate_script(keys=[key], args=[window_s]) <= limit\n\nRELEASE_LUA = \"\"\"\nif redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then return redis.call('DEL', KEYS[1]) end\nreturn 0\n\"\"\"\nrelease_script = r.register_script(RELEASE_LUA)\n\n@contextmanager\ndef with_lock(name, ttl_ms=30000):\n    token = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    if not r.set(f\"lock:{name}\", token, nx=True, px=ttl_ms):\n        raise RuntimeError(\"busy\")\n    try:\n        yield                                  # with with_lock(\"reindex:tenant42\"): ...\n    finally:\n        release_script(keys=[f\"lock:{name}\"], args=[token])   # only deletes if still ours\n```","tradeoffs":["Sub-millisecond operations in memory versus a dataset that must fit in RAM; when the working set exceeds memory, either eviction or a move to a disk-based store is forced.","AOF with fsync every write approaches database durability but cuts throughput; fsync every second is the usual compromise, accepting up to a second of loss on a crash.","Lua scripts and `MULTI` give atomicity across commands, but a long script blocks every other client; the trade flips to application-side logic when the script would touch thousands of keys.","Cluster mode scales memory and throughput across nodes, but multi-key operations require hash tags and cross-slot transactions are unavailable, which complicates scripts written for a single node.","Expiring locks never deadlock after a crash, but they can be lost by a slow holder; adding fencing tokens restores safety at the cost of making the protected resource check the token.","Using Redis as a queue is simple and fast, but asynchronous replication and eviction can drop jobs; when loss is unacceptable, a durable broker or a database-backed queue is the better trade."],"pitfalls":["Running `KEYS *` or a huge `SMEMBERS` in production; the symptom is every client stalling for seconds while the single thread walks the keyspace. Use `SCAN`.","Caching without a TTL or a sensible eviction policy; the symptom is memory growth until `maxmemory` and then either write failures (`noeviction`) or surprising evictions of non-cache keys.","Sharing one instance between cache and queue; the symptom is jobs vanishing under memory pressure because `allkeys-lru` evicted them.","Setting a lock without an expiry; the symptom is a permanent \"busy\" after one worker crashes mid-task.","Treating a lock with expiry as exclusive without fencing; the symptom is two workers writing the same resource after a GC pause or slow model call exceeded the TTL.","Hot keys and large values (a 5 MB cached response read 1,000 times a second); the symptom is one node saturating its network while others idle.","Opening a new connection per request instead of pooling; the symptom is connection-count spikes and latency dominated by handshakes.","Cache stampede when a popular key expires; the symptom is a burst of identical provider calls at the same second. Use a short lock or early refresh on the miss path."],"when":"Use Redis for state that is shared across processes, read-heavy or counter-shaped, and rebuildable or\ntolerable to lose: caches, rate limits, semaphores, sessions, short-lived coordination, and simple job\nqueues where occasional loss is acceptable. Do not use it as the system of record for conversations,\nbilling or anything you cannot recompute; use PostgreSQL for that and put Redis in front. Before adding\nit, confirm that the database genuinely cannot carry the load (a rate limit on a few hundred requests per\nsecond may be fine in PostgreSQL). Watch memory usage, evicted keys, hit ratio and slow-log entries. In\ninterviews, be ready to explain why single-threaded execution makes `INCR` a correct distributed counter,\nand where an expiring lock is unsafe.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements cache-aside, rate limiting and semaphores with atomic commands or Lua, sets TTLs on every key, and pools connections in FastAPI or Node services.","architect":"Decides what may live only in Redis versus the database, the eviction policy and persistence mode per instance, and whether the queue needs a durable broker instead.","fde":"Checks a customer's managed Redis tier, memory ceiling and eviction policy before deploying a cache or limiter, and explains what is lost on failover of their setup."},"check":{"q":"A team uses one Redis instance with `allkeys-lru` for both the LLM response cache and the Celery\njob queue. Jobs occasionally disappear under load. Explain the mechanism and the fix.","a":"Under memory pressure `allkeys-lru` evicts whichever keys were least recently used, and a queued\njob that has been waiting is exactly that: untouched since it was enqueued. The cache fills memory with\nfresh entries, the eviction policy removes the oldest keys, and some of those are queue entries or task\nmetadata, so jobs vanish without an error. The fix is separation: a dedicated instance (or at least a\nseparate logical database with its own memory policy) for the queue configured with `noeviction` and\nAOF persistence, so running out of memory fails enqueues loudly instead of silently discarding work, and\na cache instance with `allkeys-lru` and TTLs on every key. If loss is still unacceptable, move the queue\nto a durable broker or a database-backed queue."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a plain INCR in Redis be used as a correct rate-limit counter across many application servers?","options":["Redis replicates the increment to every application server's memory","Commands execute one at a time on a single thread, so each INCR is atomic without client-side locking","INCR acquires a row lock like a relational UPDATE","The client library retries INCR until the value is consistent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Serial execution means no two increments interleave, so the returned count is exact. A memcached-style get-then-set would race between servers; INCR's atomicity is a property of Redis's execution model."},{"q":"A lock is acquired with SET key token NX PX 30000. What problem remains if the holder takes longer than 30 seconds?","options":["The lock becomes permanent and other workers block forever","The token is rotated automatically and the holder cannot release it","The key expires, another worker acquires the lock, and two holders can act on the resource at once","Redis returns an error to the original holder on its next command"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Expiry protects against crashed holders but not slow ones. Once the key is gone a second worker acquires it; only a fencing token checked by the protected resource makes this safe."},{"q":"Which persistence and eviction configuration is appropriate for a Redis instance used as a job queue?","options":["RDB snapshots every hour with allkeys-lru","No persistence with volatile-lru, since jobs are short-lived","AOF persistence with noeviction, so a full instance fails writes rather than discarding jobs","AOF persistence with allkeys-lru, so old jobs make room for new ones"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A queue must fail loudly, never drop work silently. noeviction makes memory exhaustion an error the producer sees, and AOF limits what a crash loses; LRU policies would evict waiting jobs first."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/postgresql-transactions-and-isolation-levels","sources":["Official docs: PostgreSQL","Berenson et al., 1995 — A Critique of ANSI SQL Isolation Levels","Ports and Grittner, 2012 — Serializable Snapshot Isolation in PostgreSQL"],"why":"Backends run many requests at once against the same rows. Without transactions, a crash between \"insert\nthe message\" and \"increment the tenant's token counter\" leaves the two permanently inconsistent, and\nwithout isolation, two requests that both read a balance, compute a new one and write it back will\noverwrite each other and lose one of the updates. These are not rare races: a budget check before an\nLLM call, a counter of concurrent runs, \"create the conversation if it does not exist\", and \"claim the\nnext job\" are all read-then-write sequences that run concurrently in any real service.\n\nThe naive defence, a global lock in the application, serializes everything and does not survive a second\nserver. The classic database answer, two-phase locking, makes readers and writers block each other, so a\nlong report query stalls every insert. PostgreSQL instead keeps multiple versions of each row and gives\neach transaction a consistent snapshot, which lets readers and writers proceed concurrently. The\nremaining question, which anomalies a snapshot still permits, is what isolation levels answer, and it is\nwhere most production data-corruption bugs in otherwise careful code come from. Understanding the\nlevels is cheaper than debugging a tenant whose token usage no longer adds up.","how":"### ACID in one paragraph\nA transaction (`BEGIN` ... `COMMIT`) is **atomic**: all of its writes become visible together or none do,\nvia rollback. **Consistency** means constraints hold at commit. **Isolation** is the subject of this\nlesson. **Durability** means a committed transaction survives a crash, which PostgreSQL achieves by\nwriting to the write-ahead log and fsyncing it before acknowledging the commit.\n\n### MVCC: versions instead of locks\nEvery row version carries the id of the transaction that created it (`xmin`) and, once updated or\ndeleted, the one that ended it (`xmax`). An `UPDATE` does not overwrite; it writes a new version and\nmarks the old one as ended. A transaction takes a **snapshot** (the set of transaction ids that were\ncommitted when it started looking) and sees exactly the versions that were committed in that snapshot.\nThis is why a long `SELECT` never blocks an `UPDATE` and vice versa. Old versions become dead once no\nsnapshot can see them, and `VACUUM` reclaims them, which is why long-running or idle-in-transaction\nsessions cause table bloat: they pin old snapshots and prevent cleanup.\n\n### The isolation levels in PostgreSQL\nPostgreSQL offers three distinct behaviours (READ UNCOMMITTED is accepted but behaves as READ COMMITTED,\nbecause MVCC never exposes uncommitted data):\n\n1. **READ COMMITTED (default).** Each *statement* takes a fresh snapshot, so a transaction can see\n   different data in two consecutive selects if another transaction committed in between\n   (non-repeatable read). An `UPDATE` that finds its target row changed by a concurrent committed\n   transaction waits, then re-evaluates its `WHERE` on the new version and proceeds. This is fast and\n   rarely aborts, but a read-modify-write in application code is not protected at all.\n2. **REPEATABLE READ.** The snapshot is taken at the first statement and held for the whole transaction,\n   so repeated reads agree and, in PostgreSQL, phantoms do not appear either. If the transaction tries to\n   update a row that another transaction modified and committed since the snapshot, it fails with\n   serialization error `40001` and must be retried. It still permits **write skew**: two transactions\n   each read a condition, both see it true, each writes a *different* row, and the combined result\n   violates the condition.\n3. **SERIALIZABLE.** Serializable snapshot isolation (SSI) tracks read/write dependencies between\n   concurrent transactions and aborts one when a cycle would make the outcome non-serializable. The\n   result is as if transactions ran one at a time, with no explicit locking, at the cost of extra\n   bookkeeping and more `40001` aborts under contention. Any code using it must retry.\n\n### The lost update, concretely\nTenant budget 1,000,000 tokens, 999,000 used. Two requests costing 800 each run `SELECT tokens_used`,\nboth read 999,000, both compute 999,800 ≤ 1,000,000, both `UPDATE tokens_used = 999800`. Final value:\n999,800, real usage 1,000,600, and the budget was exceeded without any error. Under READ COMMITTED this is\nfully allowed. Three fixes, each with a different cost:\n- **Atomic update:** `UPDATE tenants SET tokens_used = tokens_used + 800 WHERE id = $1 AND\n  tokens_used + 800 <= budget RETURNING tokens_used`. The check and the write are one statement that\n  waits for the row lock, re-evaluates, and returns zero rows if the budget is now blown. Cheapest, works\n  at any level.\n- **Pessimistic lock:** `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` locks the row for the transaction, so the second request\n  waits until the first commits, then reads the new value. Simple, but the lock is held for the whole\n  transaction, so never hold it across a network call.\n- **Optimistic:** read a `version` column, write with `WHERE version = $old`, and retry when zero rows\n  are affected; or run the transaction at SERIALIZABLE and retry on `40001`.\n\n### Row locks, SKIP LOCKED and deadlocks\n`FOR UPDATE` and the weaker `FOR NO KEY UPDATE` / `FOR SHARE` lock individual rows. `FOR UPDATE SKIP\nLOCKED` returns only rows nobody else holds, which is the primitive behind database-backed job queues:\nmany workers each claim a different row with one statement. `NOWAIT` errors instead of waiting. When two\ntransactions each hold a lock the other wants, PostgreSQL detects the cycle after `deadlock_timeout`\n(one second by default) and aborts one of them with `40P01`; the usual cause is acquiring locks in\ndifferent orders, and the usual fix is a consistent ordering (sort ids before locking).\n\n### Things that surprise people\n- After any error inside a transaction, PostgreSQL rejects every further statement until `ROLLBACK`\n  (or rollback to a `SAVEPOINT`). Catching the exception in code and continuing does not help.\n- Transaction-level connection poolers (PgBouncer in transaction mode) reuse server connections between\n  transactions, so session state such as `SET`, advisory locks and prepared statements leaks or vanishes.\n- Isolation level is per transaction: `BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE` or `SET TRANSACTION` as the\n  first statement; a `SET default_transaction_isolation` changes the session default.","example":"The budget race above, written with psycopg (3.x). `reserve_atomic` is the single-statement fix.\n`reserve_serializable` shows the SERIALIZABLE-plus-retry pattern for logic that genuinely needs several\nstatements, such as checking a budget and then inserting the run that consumes it.\n\n```python\nimport time, psycopg\nfrom psycopg.errors import SerializationFailure\n\nDSN = \"postgresql://app@localhost/app\"\n\ndef reserve_atomic(conn, tenant_id, cost):\n    row = conn.execute(\n        \"\"\"UPDATE tenants SET tokens_used = tokens_used + %s\n           WHERE id = %s AND tokens_used + %s <= budget\n           RETURNING tokens_used\"\"\", (cost, tenant_id, cost)).fetchone()\n    return row is not None            # None means the budget would be exceeded\n\ndef reserve_serializable(conn, tenant_id, cost, run_id, attempts=5):\n    for attempt in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            with conn.transaction():\n                conn.execute(\"SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE\")\n                used, budget = conn.execute(\n                    \"SELECT tokens_used, budget FROM tenants WHERE id = %s\",\n                    (tenant_id,)).fetchone()\n                if used + cost > budget:\n                    return False\n                conn.execute(\"UPDATE tenants SET tokens_used = %s WHERE id = %s\",\n                             (used + cost, tenant_id))\n                conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO runs (id, tenant_id, cost) VALUES (%s, %s, %s)\",\n                             (run_id, tenant_id, cost))   # unique id makes retries idempotent\n                return True\n        except SerializationFailure:      # SQLSTATE 40001: another txn won; retry\n            time.sleep(0.01 * 2 ** attempt)\n    raise RuntimeError(\"could not reserve budget after retries\")\n\nwith psycopg.connect(DSN) as conn:\n    ok = reserve_atomic(conn, tenant_id=42, cost=800)\n    conn.commit()\n    print(\"reserved\" if ok else \"over budget\")\n```\n\nRun two copies of either function concurrently against a tenant at 999,000 of 1,000,000: the atomic\nversion lets exactly one through; the naive select-then-update version lets both through.","tradeoffs":["READ COMMITTED gives high throughput and almost no aborts, but every read-modify-write in application code is a race; it flips to a stricter level or explicit locks as soon as money or quotas are involved.","SERIALIZABLE guarantees correctness without reasoning about every race, but aborts rise with contention and every caller must implement retries; on a hot counter row it can thrash.","`FOR UPDATE` is simple and never aborts, but it blocks other writers for the length of the transaction, so a transaction that also calls an LLM holds the row for seconds.","Optimistic version columns avoid locks and work through a pooler, but under heavy contention most attempts fail and the retry loop becomes the bottleneck.","Long transactions give a consistent view for reports, but they pin old row versions, block `VACUUM` and cause bloat; the trade flips toward a replica or a snapshot export for long analytics.","Transaction-mode pooling multiplies the clients one database can serve, but costs session features such as advisory locks and `SET`, which must be moved into the transaction."],"pitfalls":["Select, compute, update in application code under the default level; the symptom is counters and balances that drift from the sum of their events, with no errors in the logs.","Holding a transaction (and its row locks) across an LLM or HTTP call; the symptom is lock waits and idle-in-transaction sessions that scale with provider latency, then pool exhaustion.","Not retrying on `40001` at REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE; the symptom is sporadic 500s under load that disappear when you retry by hand.","Assuming REPEATABLE READ prevents write skew; the symptom is an invariant spanning two rows (for example \"at most one active run per tenant\") violated by concurrent transactions that each saw it satisfied.","Catching an exception mid-transaction and continuing; the symptom is `current transaction is aborted, commands ignored until end of transaction block` on the next statement.","Deadlocks from locking rows in different orders in two code paths; the symptom is intermittent `40P01` errors roughly one second after the conflicting requests start.","Expecting READ UNCOMMITTED to behave as in other databases; in PostgreSQL it is READ COMMITTED.","Forgetting that a pooler in transaction mode breaks `SET` and advisory locks across statements; the symptom is settings that seem to apply randomly to other requests."],"when":"Start from the default level and make every write path safe by construction: atomic single-statement\nupdates for counters and quotas, `FOR UPDATE` when a multi-statement decision must be exclusive and the\ntransaction stays short, `SKIP LOCKED` for job claiming, and SERIALIZABLE with retries for genuinely\nmulti-row invariants that are hard to lock explicitly. Keep transactions short and never span a network\ncall. Watch `pg_stat_activity` for idle-in-transaction sessions, lock waits, and serialization failures\nper second. In interviews, expect to be given a read-modify-write and asked which level or lock fixes it,\nto explain write skew, and to say why a long transaction slows down a table it never wrote to.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the atomic update or the `FOR UPDATE` block, adds the `40001` retry loop, and keeps the transaction boundary inside the request rather than around the model call.","architect":"Sets the policy: which invariants are enforced by the database versus the application, the default isolation level, and the rule that transactions never cross service or provider calls.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's drifting usage counters or lock pileups from `pg_stat_activity`, and explains why their pooler configuration broke session-level settings."},"check":{"q":"A service enforces \"at most three concurrent runs per tenant\" by counting active runs in a\nREPEATABLE READ transaction, then inserting a new run if the count is below three. Under load, tenants\nend up with five active runs. Why, and what are two fixes?","a":"This is write skew. Each transaction's snapshot shows two active runs, each inserts a *new* row\nrather than updating a row the other touched, so neither conflicts with the other at REPEATABLE READ and\nboth commit, giving four or five runs. Fix one: run the transaction at SERIALIZABLE and retry on `40001`;\nSSI detects the read-write dependency between the count and the inserts and aborts one. Fix two: make\nthe conflict explicit by locking something shared, for example `SELECT ... FROM tenants WHERE id = $1\nFOR UPDATE` before counting, or a per-tenant advisory lock, so the second transaction waits and recounts\nafter the first commits. A third option is to keep an `active_runs` counter on the tenant row and use a\nsingle conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE active_runs < 3`, which turns the invariant into one atomic\nstatement and avoids retries entirely."},"quiz":[{"q":"Under PostgreSQL's default READ COMMITTED level, why can two transactions both pass a \"balance is sufficient\" check and overspend?","options":["READ COMMITTED allows reading uncommitted data from the other transaction","Each transaction reads the committed balance, computes a new value, and writes it; neither read locks the row, so both writes succeed and one update is lost","The database merges both updates by adding them together","READ COMMITTED serializes writes but not reads, so the second write is silently dropped"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A plain SELECT takes no lock and the UPDATE only waits for a concurrent writer of the same row. Both transactions read the same committed value, so the later write overwrites the earlier one."},{"q":"What does SERIALIZABLE in PostgreSQL require from application code that READ COMMITTED does not?","options":["Explicit FOR UPDATE locks on every row that will be modified","A single connection per transaction without pooling","A retry loop, because the database may abort a transaction with a serialization failure when a conflict is detected","Disabling MVCC so that writers block readers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"SSI resolves dangerous dependency cycles by aborting one transaction with SQLSTATE 40001. The outcome is correct only if the caller retries; code that treats the abort as a fatal error turns safety into 500s."},{"q":"Why does an idle session that opened a transaction an hour ago slow down tables it never touched?","options":["It holds an exclusive lock on the whole database","Its snapshot keeps old row versions alive, so VACUUM cannot reclaim dead tuples and tables bloat","It consumes all available connections in the pool","PostgreSQL pauses checkpoints while any transaction is open"],"answer":1,"explanation":"MVCC keeps a row version until no snapshot can see it. An old open snapshot pins dead versions everywhere, so tables and indexes grow and scans read more pages; this is why idle-in-transaction is monitored."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/message-queues","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Apache Kafka","Official docs: Amazon SQS","Official docs: RabbitMQ"],"why":"A request that triggers five downstream actions (store the upload, extract text, embed chunks, update\nthe index, notify the user) cannot do them inline: the HTTP call would take minutes, any one failure\nfails the whole request, and a burst of uploads would hit every downstream service at the burst rate.\nCalling the services synchronously couples their availability (if the embedder is down, uploads fail),\ntheir capacity (the slowest service sets the pace), and their deployment (a change in one means retries\nacross all). The naive fix, \"retry in a background thread\", loses the work when the process restarts and\nstill has no buffer when the downstream is slower than the upstream.\n\nA queue solves the three couplings at once. Producers write a message and return; the broker stores it\ndurably; consumers pull at their own rate; if a consumer is down the messages wait. The same mechanism\ngives fan-out (several consumer groups read the same event), load levelling (a burst becomes a backlog\nthat drains), and a natural place to put retries and dead-letter handling. In AI systems the pattern\nappears constantly: document ingestion pipelines, batch embedding, webhook buffering from providers,\ndecoupling an agent's tool calls from the services that execute them, and emitting events (run started,\ntool failed, tokens consumed) that observability and billing consume independently.\n\nThe price is that the system becomes asynchronous and eventually consistent, and that every consumer\nmust tolerate seeing the same message twice.","how":"### The core loop\nA producer publishes a message to a named destination (queue, topic, exchange). The broker persists it\nand makes it available. A consumer receives it, does the work, and **acknowledges** it; only then does the\nbroker consider it done. If the consumer crashes before acknowledging, the broker redelivers to another\nconsumer. This ack-after-work protocol is what makes delivery **at-least-once**: a consumer that finishes\nthe work and dies before the ack causes a duplicate. Acknowledging *before* the work gives\n**at-most-once**: no duplicates, but a crash loses the message. \"Exactly-once\" across arbitrary side\neffects is not achievable; what systems offer is at-least-once delivery plus **idempotent** consumers\n(deduplicate by message id or business key), which is effectively-once from the outside.\n\n### Ordering, partitions and parallelism\nA single queue consumed by one worker is ordered; the moment you add workers, messages complete out of\norder. Kafka's answer is **partitions**: a topic is split into N ordered logs, a message's key decides its\npartition (same key, same partition, strict order), and a **consumer group** assigns each partition to\nexactly one consumer in the group. Parallelism is therefore capped by partition count, and a skewed key\n(one huge tenant) makes one partition hot. SQS FIFO queues offer the same idea with message group ids;\nSQS standard queues and RabbitMQ classic queues give best-effort ordering only.\n\n### Backpressure and retries\nA queue only levels load if someone watches the **lag** (messages waiting, or the age of the oldest\nmessage); an unbounded backlog is a silent outage. Consumers limit in-flight messages (RabbitMQ\n`prefetch`, SQS `MaxNumberOfMessages`, Kafka `max.poll.records`). Failed messages are retried with\nbackoff, and after N attempts go to a **dead-letter queue** (DLQ) so one poison message cannot block the\nrest. A message whose payload is large (a PDF, a transcript) should not travel through the broker:\nstore it in object storage and send the reference (the claim-check pattern).\n\n### Kafka: a distributed, replayable log\nKafka keeps messages on disk for a configured retention (time or size), regardless of whether they were\nconsumed. Consumers track their own **offset** per partition and commit it; replaying means resetting the\noffset. Durability comes from replicating each partition to several brokers and, for producers,\n`acks=all` plus the idempotent producer setting, which deduplicates retries within a session. Log\ncompaction keeps the latest message per key, turning a topic into a changelog. Kafka is built for high\nthroughput, many consumers of the same stream, and event sourcing; it costs an operations burden (or a\nmanaged service) and a rigid partition-based parallelism model. It is a poor fit for a small job queue\nwhere each job needs individual retry and priority.\n\n### SQS: a managed queue without replay\nSQS is a fully managed queue: no brokers to run, near-unlimited throughput on standard queues, and\npay-per-request. A consumer receives messages with a **visibility timeout**; the message is hidden from\nothers until the consumer deletes it or the timeout expires, at which point it reappears. Long polling\nwaits up to 20 seconds for messages instead of returning empty. Retention is bounded (days, not forever),\nmessages are deleted when acknowledged, so there is no replay; a redrive policy moves messages to a DLQ\nafter `maxReceiveCount` attempts. Standard queues are at-least-once with best-effort ordering; FIFO\nqueues add ordering per message group and a deduplication id honoured for a five-minute window. Message\nsize is small (a quarter of a megabyte historically; check current limits), so payloads go to S3.\n\n### RabbitMQ: a routing broker\nRabbitMQ implements AMQP: producers publish to an **exchange**, bindings route messages to **queues** by\nrouting key (direct), pattern (topic), to all bound queues (fanout), or by headers. This makes complex\nrouting and per-consumer queues easy. Consumers ack or nack; `prefetch` bounds in-flight messages per\nconsumer; dead-letter exchanges handle rejects and TTL expiry. Quorum queues replicate via Raft for\ndurability; the newer streams feature adds a Kafka-like replayable log. It is a good fit for task\ndistribution with rich routing and moderate throughput, and it needs operating like any stateful system.\n\n### Choosing\nNeed replay, many independent consumers of the same events, or very high throughput: Kafka (or a managed\nequivalent). Need a simple durable buffer with minimal operations on AWS: SQS. Need routing rules,\nper-message priorities or AMQP interop: RabbitMQ. Need a small job queue and already run PostgreSQL: a\ntable with `SKIP LOCKED` is often enough, and Redis-backed queues sit between that and a real broker.","example":"An SQS consumer for an embedding job. It long-polls, processes each message idempotently (the job id is\nthe dedup key stored with the result), extends the visibility timeout while a slow model call runs so the\nmessage is not redelivered mid-flight, and deletes only after success. Failures are left to expire and\nretry; the queue's redrive policy sends repeat offenders to a DLQ.\n\n```python\nimport json, threading, boto3\n\nsqs = boto3.client(\"sqs\")\nQUEUE = \"https://sqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/embed-jobs\"\n\ndef extend_visibility(receipt, stop, every=30, extra=60):\n    while not stop.wait(every):                        # heartbeat while work is in flight\n        sqs.change_message_visibility(QueueUrl=QUEUE, ReceiptHandle=receipt,\n                                      VisibilityTimeout=extra)\n\ndef process(job):\n    if results.exists(job[\"job_id\"]):                  # idempotent: seen this job already\n        return\n    vectors = embed(load_chunks(job[\"document_id\"]))   # the slow call\n    results.store(job[\"job_id\"], vectors)              # write result keyed by job id\n\nwhile True:\n    resp = sqs.receive_message(QueueUrl=QUEUE, MaxNumberOfMessages=5,\n                               WaitTimeSeconds=20, VisibilityTimeout=60)\n    for msg in resp.get(\"Messages\", []):\n        stop = threading.Event()\n        hb = threading.Thread(target=extend_visibility,\n                              args=(msg[\"ReceiptHandle\"], stop), daemon=True)\n        hb.start()\n        try:\n            process(json.loads(msg[\"Body\"]))\n            sqs.delete_message(QueueUrl=QUEUE, ReceiptHandle=msg[\"ReceiptHandle\"])\n        except Exception as e:\n            log.warning(\"job failed, will be redelivered: %s\", e)   # no delete: retry later\n        finally:\n            stop.set()\n```\n\n`results`, `embed`, `load_chunks` and `log` are the application's own; the shape of the loop is what\nmatters. With Kafka the same loop would poll a partition, process, then commit the offset; with RabbitMQ\nit would `basic_consume` with `prefetch_count=5` and `basic_ack` after success.","tradeoffs":["Decoupling producers from consumers gains availability and burst tolerance, but the caller no longer knows when (or whether) the work finished, so every flow needs a status path or an event back.","At-least-once delivery never loses acknowledged work, but it forces idempotent consumers; at-most-once is simpler and acceptable only for data you can afford to drop, such as metrics samples.","Kafka's retained log allows replay and many consumer groups, but operations cost, partition planning and the lack of per-message retry make it heavy for a plain job queue.","SQS removes all broker operations and scales without planning, but no replay, bounded retention and a small message size push payloads to S3 and audit history to a database.","RabbitMQ's exchanges give fine-grained routing and priorities, but a single very hot queue is harder to scale than Kafka partitions, and the cluster must be run and upgraded.","Partition-keyed ordering preserves per-entity sequence, but parallelism is capped by partition count and a skewed key concentrates load; the trade flips toward unordered queues when order does not matter."],"pitfalls":["Non-idempotent consumers; the symptom is duplicate emails, double charges or two embeddings per chunk after any consumer restart or timeout.","A visibility timeout or ack deadline shorter than the work (a 90-second model call under a 30-second timeout); the symptom is the same job running on two workers and redelivery counts climbing under load.","No dead-letter queue; the symptom is one malformed message redelivered forever, consuming a worker and hiding behind it a growing backlog.","Committing the Kafka offset (or deleting the SQS message) before processing; the symptom is work that silently disappears whenever a worker crashes.","Nobody alerting on queue lag or oldest-message age; the symptom is a backlog discovered hours later by a user asking why their document never got indexed.","Choosing Kafka for a few messages per second of job traffic; the symptom is a team spending its time on partition rebalances and consumer-group tuning instead of the product.","Assuming order on SQS standard queues or across Kafka partitions; the symptom is \"updated\" events processed before \"created\" events and intermittent not-found errors.","Sending multi-megabyte payloads through the broker; the symptom is size errors, slow brokers and a broker that has become an accidental blob store."],"when":"Introduce a queue when work must outlive the request, when a downstream is slower or less available than\nthe caller, when several consumers need the same events, or when bursts must be smoothed. Do not\nintroduce one for a synchronous read the user is waiting on, or when a database table with `SKIP LOCKED`\nwould carry the load without a new system. Measure lag, oldest-message age, redelivery rate and DLQ\ndepth from day one. Day to day this shows up as \"should this endpoint enqueue or call directly\", choosing\na partition key, and tuning timeouts to the real duration of an LLM call. In interviews, expect to\nexplain at-least-once, design a partition key for a tenant-skewed workload, and justify Kafka versus SQS\nfor a given scenario.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes idempotent consumers, sets visibility or ack timeouts to match model latency, wires the DLQ and alerts on lag before the first production backlog.","architect":"Chooses the broker per workload (log versus queue), defines topics, keys and retention, and decides where the status of asynchronous work is recorded for callers.","fde":"Integrates with the customer's existing Kafka or SQS, negotiates message schemas and ordering guarantees, and sizes consumers against their throughput and quota limits."},"check":{"q":"A document pipeline on SQS standard occasionally embeds the same chunk twice and sometimes\nprocesses a document's \"deleted\" event before its \"created\" event. What is happening and how would you\nchange the design?","a":"Both are expected properties of a standard queue. Duplicate embeddings come from at-least-once\ndelivery: a worker finished the model call but crashed or exceeded its visibility timeout before deleting\nthe message, so it was redelivered. The fix is an idempotent consumer: key each result by chunk id or\njob id and skip or overwrite when it already exists, and set the visibility timeout above the real\nprocessing time with heartbeat extension for long calls. The ordering problem comes from standard queues\noffering only best-effort order across many workers. Either move to a FIFO queue with the document id as\nthe message group id, so events for one document are serialized while different documents proceed in\nparallel, or make the consumer order-independent by storing a version per document and ignoring events\nolder than the current state. Add a DLQ so poison messages do not block either path."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is \"exactly-once delivery\" not something a broker can promise for a consumer that calls an external API?","options":["Brokers lose messages under load, so at-least-once is the best achievable","The consumer can complete the external call and then fail before acknowledging, so the broker cannot know whether the side effect happened and must redeliver","External APIs do not support message acknowledgements","Exactly-once requires FIFO queues, which most brokers lack"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The acknowledgement and the side effect are separate operations that cannot be made atomic across systems. Redelivery is the safe default, so idempotency on the consumer side is what makes the outcome effectively once."},{"q":"A Kafka topic has 4 partitions and a consumer group has 8 consumers. What happens?","options":["Each partition is read by two consumers for redundancy","Kafka automatically increases the partition count to 8","Four consumers sit idle, because each partition is assigned to at most one consumer in a group","Messages are round-robined across all 8 consumers regardless of partition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Parallelism within a consumer group is bounded by partition count. Extra consumers are standby only, which is why partition count must be planned for peak consumer parallelism."},{"q":"Which situation makes SQS a better choice than Kafka?","options":["Multiple independent teams need to replay the same event history","A team needs a durable buffer for background jobs with minimal operations and no need to re-read past messages","Strict ordering of every message across the whole topic is required","Messages must be retained indefinitely as the system of record"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SQS offers a managed, pay-per-use queue with retries and dead-lettering but deletes messages on acknowledgement and bounds retention. Replay, long retention and many consumer groups are Kafka's strengths."}]},{"key":"backend-fundamentals/oauth-2-0-oidc","sources":["Hardt (ed.), 2012 — The OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework (RFC 6749)","Sakimura et al., 2014 — OpenID Connect Core 1.0","Sakimura, Bradley, Agarwal, 2015 — Proof Key for Code Exchange by OAuth Public Clients (RFC 7636)","Lodderstedt et al. — OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice (IETF)"],"why":"Before OAuth, a third-party app that wanted to read your calendar asked for your Google password. That\nhands over everything, not just the calendar; it cannot be revoked per app (only by changing the\npassword, which breaks every app at once); it stops working the moment the account has MFA; and it\nmeans dozens of apps store a credential that unlocks the whole account. The naive fix, a static per-user\nAPI key, removes password sharing but keeps the rest: keys are long-lived, rarely scoped, hard to\nrotate, and the user still copies a secret between systems by hand.\n\nOAuth 2.0 (2012) restructured the problem into four roles: the **resource owner** (the user), the\n**client** (the app), the **authorization server** (which authenticates the user, records consent and\nissues tokens) and the **resource server** (the API, which accepts tokens). The user authenticates only\nto the authorization server. The client receives a token that is scoped (`calendar.readonly`),\nshort-lived (minutes to an hour), revocable, and tied to that one client.\n\nThen people started using OAuth for login: \"if the app can call the profile API with this token, the\nuser must be who the profile says\". That inference is wrong. An access token proves the *client was\nauthorized*, not *who is present at the keyboard*. An attacker can obtain a perfectly valid access token\nfor a victim through a malicious app and replay it against another app that trusts the profile endpoint\n(token substitution). OIDC (2014) exists to close exactly that hole: it defines an **ID token**, a JWT\nsigned by the identity provider with the client's own id in the audience and a nonce bound to this login\nattempt, so authentication becomes something you verify rather than something you infer.","how":"### Two channels, one principle\nThe **front channel** is the user's browser: untrusted, logged, visible in history and referrers. The\n**back channel** is a TLS connection from the client's server to the authorization server. The design\nrule that explains most of the protocol is *nothing valuable travels the front channel*. The browser\nonly ever carries a short-lived, single-use authorization code; tokens move on the back channel.\n\n### Authorization code flow with PKCE, step by step\n1. The client generates three random values: `state` (binds the callback to this browser session,\n   defeating login CSRF), `nonce` (OIDC: binds the ID token to this attempt) and a PKCE pair, a\n   `code_verifier` and `code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier))`. It stores all three in the\n   user's server-side session.\n2. It redirects the browser to the `/authorize` endpoint with `response_type=code`, `client_id`,\n   `redirect_uri`, `scope=openid profile email …`, `state`, `nonce`, `code_challenge` and\n   `code_challenge_method=S256`.\n3. The authorization server authenticates the user however it likes (password, passkey, MFA, corporate\n   SSO); the client sees none of it. It shows a consent screen listing the scopes.\n4. It redirects back to `redirect_uri?code=…&state=…`. The client checks `state` matches the session.\n5. **Back channel.** The client POSTs `code`, `redirect_uri`, `code_verifier` and, for a confidential\n   client, its `client_secret` to `/token`. The server checks the code is unused and unexpired, that\n   `SHA256(code_verifier)` equals the challenge from step 2, and that `redirect_uri` matches the one\n   registered. PKCE exists *because* public clients (SPAs, mobile apps, CLIs) cannot keep a secret and a\n   code can be intercepted (a malicious app registering the same custom URL scheme, a proxy log). Binding\n   the code to a value the interceptor never saw makes the stolen code worthless. The current IETF\n   security best-practice recommends PKCE for confidential clients too, since it also stops code injection.\n6. The response carries `access_token`, `token_type`, `expires_in`, optionally `refresh_token`, and,\n   when the `openid` scope was requested, `id_token`.\n\n### Three tokens, three audiences\n- **Access token.** For the resource server; its audience is the API. It may be opaque or a JWT, and\n  the client must treat it as opaque either way. Sent as `Authorization: Bearer …`. The API validates it:\n  for a JWT, signature against the issuer's JWKS plus `iss`, `aud`, `exp` and the required scopes; for an\n  opaque token, a call to the introspection endpoint (RFC 7662).\n- **ID token.** For the client; always a JWT. Claims: `iss`, `sub` (the stable user identifier; key\n  your user table on this, not on email, which can change or be re-issued), `aud` (must equal your\n  `client_id`), `exp`, `iat`, `nonce`, `auth_time`, and optionally `email`, `name`. Never send it to an\n  API as a credential; its audience is you.\n- **Refresh token.** Lets the client get fresh access tokens without the user present. It is the\n  longest-lived secret in the system and therefore the most valuable to steal. Mitigations: rotation\n  (each use returns a new one and reuse of an old one revokes the whole family), sender-constraining\n  (DPoP or mTLS), and keeping it server-side only.\n\n### Discovery and key rotation\nAn OIDC provider publishes `/.well-known/openid-configuration` listing its endpoints and a `jwks_uri`.\nThe JWKS holds public signing keys with a `kid`. Clients cache the set and refetch on an unknown `kid`,\nwhich is how a provider rotates signing keys without breaking anyone.\n\n### The other grants\n- **Client credentials.** No user. A service authenticates as itself (secret, private-key JWT or mTLS)\n  and gets a token carrying its own identity. Use it for batch jobs and for agents acting as themselves.\n- **Device code.** For TVs and CLIs that cannot host a browser: the device polls while the user\n  approves on a phone.\n- **Deprecated.** The implicit grant (token in the URL fragment, front channel) and the resource-owner\n  password grant (the client sees the password) are both removed in the OAuth 2.1 draft. Do not build\n  anything new on them.\n\n### Scopes are not permissions\nA scope is the upper bound the *user* granted the *client*. The resource server still applies the user's\nown authorization underneath: `scope=repo` never lets an app delete a repository the user could not\ndelete themselves. Both checks run on every request (see RBAC).","example":"A confidential web client (server-side) doing the authorization code flow with PKCE and validating\nthe ID token against the provider's JWKS. The pieces that matter are the three random values, the\nback-channel exchange, and the claims that are checked before trusting `sub`.\n\n```python\nimport base64, hashlib, secrets, urllib.parse, requests, jwt   # pip install requests pyjwt[crypto]\n\nISSUER, CLIENT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET = \"https://login.example.com\", \"my-web-app\", \"<from secrets manager>\"\nREDIRECT_URI = \"https://app.example.com/callback\"\ndisc = requests.get(f\"{ISSUER}/.well-known/openid-configuration\").json()   # endpoints + jwks_uri\n\ndef start_login(session):            # 1. PKCE pair, state, nonce -> stored server-side, never in the URL\n    verifier = secrets.token_urlsafe(64)\n    challenge = base64.urlsafe_b64encode(hashlib.sha256(verifier.encode()).digest()).rstrip(b\"=\").decode()\n    session.update(verifier=verifier, state=secrets.token_urlsafe(16), nonce=secrets.token_urlsafe(16))\n    return disc[\"authorization_endpoint\"] + \"?\" + urllib.parse.urlencode({\n        \"response_type\": \"code\", \"client_id\": CLIENT_ID, \"redirect_uri\": REDIRECT_URI,\n        \"scope\": \"openid profile email\", \"state\": session[\"state\"], \"nonce\": session[\"nonce\"],\n        \"code_challenge\": challenge, \"code_challenge_method\": \"S256\"})\n\ndef handle_callback(code, returned_state, session):   # 2. browser returns ?code=...&state=...\n    if returned_state != session[\"state\"]:\n        raise PermissionError(\"state mismatch: login CSRF or stale session\")\n    tok = requests.post(disc[\"token_endpoint\"], data={             # back channel, TLS, server-to-server\n        \"grant_type\": \"authorization_code\", \"code\": code, \"redirect_uri\": REDIRECT_URI,\n        \"client_id\": CLIENT_ID, \"client_secret\": CLIENT_SECRET,\n        \"code_verifier\": session[\"verifier\"]}).json()\n    # 3. Validate the ID token: signature via JWKS, pinned algorithm, issuer, audience = our client_id, expiry\n    key = jwt.PyJWKClient(disc[\"jwks_uri\"]).get_signing_key_from_jwt(tok[\"id_token\"])\n    claims = jwt.decode(tok[\"id_token\"], key.key, algorithms=[\"RS256\"],\n                        audience=CLIENT_ID, issuer=disc[\"issuer\"])\n    if claims.get(\"nonce\") != session[\"nonce\"]:\n        raise PermissionError(\"nonce mismatch: token not minted for this login\")\n    user_id = claims[\"sub\"]                       # stable identifier; email is a display attribute\n    return user_id, tok[\"access_token\"], tok.get(\"refresh_token\")   # keep tokens server-side\n```\n\nAfter `handle_callback` the app issues its own HttpOnly session cookie; the access token is used only\nwhen the backend calls the provider's APIs on the user's behalf, and the refresh token never leaves\nthe server.","tradeoffs":["Delegation without password sharing, paid for with redirect complexity, more moving parts and an authorization server on your login path; it flips to \"a session cookie is enough\" for an internal tool that talks only to its own backend and never to third parties.","JWT access tokens let a resource server validate locally with no network call, but cannot be revoked before `exp`; opaque tokens with introspection revoke instantly at the cost of a call (or short cache) per request. Choose by how long a stolen token may stay valid.","Short-lived access tokens shrink the damage from a leak, but move the risk onto the refresh token and the session store; without rotation and server-side storage you have only relocated the problem.","An external identity provider (Okta, Entra ID, Google, Auth0) takes passwords, MFA and recovery out of your codebase, but adds a vendor on the critical path and provider-specific quirks in claim names and refresh policies. For B2B customers SSO is a requirement, so this is rarely a real choice."],"pitfalls":["Treating an access token as proof of identity by calling `/userinfo` and trusting the result; the symptom is that a token issued to app A logs the same user into app B. Require an ID token and check `aud` and `nonce`.","Omitting or not checking `state`; the symptom is login CSRF, where a victim's browser completes the attacker's login and the victim then uploads data into the attacker's account.","Keying users on `email` instead of `sub`; the symptom is duplicate accounts after a rename, or account takeover at providers that re-issue addresses.","Decoding the JWT without pinning `algorithms`, `audience` and `issuer`; the symptom is that any token from the same provider for any other app is accepted, or that an `alg: none` token passes.","Storing tokens in browser `localStorage`; the symptom is that one XSS exfiltrates every user's tokens. Use a backend-for-frontend with an HttpOnly session cookie and keep tokens server-side.","Handing an agent or integration a broad, long-lived refresh token \"to be safe\"; the symptom is one compromised worker that can act as every user. Request minimal scopes, and use client credentials for work the agent does as itself."],"when":"Reach for OIDC whenever humans log in and you do not want to own passwords, MFA and account recovery,\nwhich for B2B products is nearly always: \"do you support SSO\" appears in the first security\nquestionnaire. Reach for the OAuth authorization code grant when your product must call APIs as the user\n(Google Drive, GitHub, Slack, a CRM for an agent), and for client credentials when a service calls\nanother service. Skip it for a single internal app on a session cookie, and never use it as a substitute\nfor \"the cron job needs a credential\". Day to day the work is token validation middleware, mapping\nprovider claims to your roles, token storage and rotation, and provider quirks. In interviews, be able to\ndraw the code flow, explain why the exchange happens on the back channel, what PKCE prevents, and what\neach of the three tokens is for and who validates it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the callback, validates ID tokens against the JWKS, stores refresh tokens server-side, and writes the bearer middleware that checks issuer, audience, expiry and scope on every API request.","architect":"Decides client types and grants per surface (backend-for-frontend for SPAs, client credentials for workers, delegated user tokens for agents), picks JWT versus opaque access tokens by revocation needs, and owns the identity-provider boundary and the claim-to-role mapping.","fde":"Wires the product into the customer's Okta or Entra tenant, debugs redirect-URI and claim mismatches, gets the agent's tool integrations consented with minimal scopes, and explains why the agent acts with the user's token rather than a shared admin key."},"check":{"q":"A single-page app logs users in with Google by obtaining an access token in the browser, calling\nthe userinfo endpoint, and posting the returned email to the backend, which trusts it. What is wrong,\nand how would you redesign it?","a":"Three independent failures. First, identity is inferred from an access token: a token minted for\nany other app for the same user, or an attacker's own token, is accepted, because nothing ties it to this\nclient; the fix is to require an ID token and validate signature, `iss`, `aud` equal to your client id,\n`exp` and `nonce`. Second, tokens live in JavaScript, so a single XSS steals them. Third, the backend\ntrusts a client-asserted email. Redesign as a backend-for-frontend: the browser hits `/login`, the server\nbuilds the authorize URL with `state`, `nonce` and PKCE, handles the callback on the back channel,\nvalidates the ID token, keys the user on `sub`, and sets an HttpOnly session cookie. Access and refresh\ntokens stay server-side and are used only for calls to Google on the user's behalf, with refresh-token\nrotation enabled."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the authorization code flow send a code through the browser and exchange it server-side instead of returning the access token directly?","options":["Codes are shorter than tokens and fit more reliably in a redirect URL","The browser is an untrusted channel; a single-use code bound to PKCE or a client secret is useless if intercepted, whereas a token would be directly usable","Access tokens can only be issued over a connection authenticated with mTLS","It lets the authorization server rate-limit how many tokens a client receives"],"answer":1,"explanation":"URLs leak through history, logs and referrers. A code that can only be redeemed with the verifier or secret the interceptor lacks limits the damage; the implicit grant that put tokens in the URL was deprecated for precisely this reason."},{"q":"A mobile app calls its own backend API and also needs to know who the user is. Which token goes where?","options":["The ID token to the API, and the access token to establish identity","The access token to the API, and the ID token to establish identity; each has a different audience","The refresh token to the API, and the ID token to establish identity","Either token works for both purposes because both are signed JWTs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The ID token's audience is the client, the access token's audience is the API. Sending the ID token to an API or using an access token as identity proof breaks the audience check that prevents token substitution between applications."},{"q":"What does PKCE protect against that a client secret alone cannot?","options":["Brute-force guessing of the user's password at the authorization server","Replay of an access token after it has expired","Interception of the authorization code by or on behalf of a public client that cannot hold a secret, and code injection generally","Forging the signature of an ID token"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A public client ships its code to users, so any embedded secret is public. PKCE binds the code to a per-request verifier that never travels the front channel, so an intercepted code cannot be redeemed; the IETF best-practice now recommends it for all clients."}]}]});
